{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P5rw2MG8PtbagEGecpYSn4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# The 3D engine and the 3D kits\n2\t\n3\t`engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (v0.34.0) puts a real 3D world under a my-3D2dge game: a level written as text (or an open world's terrain), its physics,\n4\tits characters, its camera and its picture, drawn by three.js r182 and simulated by Rapier 0.19.3. It is the stress\n5\ttest's 3D world (the 3D Prototype, deleted in v0.51.4; `docs/LAB-3D.md`) taken apart and made general, so that a game can be built on it the\n6\tway the 2D starter kits are built on the engine. Its header is its manual: an agent reads that and the API card, never\n7\tthe code below them.\n8\t\n9\t| What | Where |\n10\t| --- | --- |\n11\t| The 3D engine (an ES module) | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`; built copy `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` |\n12\t| Its manual | its header (the `/*! ... */` comment), with a complete quick-start game; a budget of 8,000 tokens (D27, D60) |\n13\t| The 3D starter: a title stage and every 3D slice | `src/starter3d/` and `src/starter3d.template.html`, built into `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.html` ([/3d](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d)) |\n14\t| The 3D kits, one slice each | `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-action.html` ([/kits/3d-action](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/3d-action)), `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-platformer.html` ([/kits/3d-platformer](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/3d-platformer)), `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-shooter.html` ([/kits/3d-shooter](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/3d-shooter)), `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-horror.html` ([/kits/3d-horror](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/3d-horror)) |\n15\t| The check | `node tools/kit3d-test.mjs` (in `npm test`) |\n16\t| The libraries | `vendor/three-0.182.0/`, `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` (pinned, with checksums: `node tools/vendor-3d.mjs --check`) |\n17\t\n18\t## How it fits the 2D engine\n19\t\n20\tThe 2D engine still runs the game. `new E.Game({ ..., bg: null })` makes its canvas clear (new in v0.34.0), so the\n21\tgame's HUD, menus, notes and touch buttons draw over the 3D picture; everything a 2D game gets comes with it: the loop of\n22\texact fixed steps, scenes, input with rebinding and touch buttons, the pause menu and settings, full screen, the boot\n23\tscreen, sound. The 3D engine adds `E.World3D` (and `E.THREE`, `E.TSL`, `E.RAPIER`):\n24\t\n25\t- **The level is text**, one character per 1-metre tile, as the stress test's hall was: walls, raised floors, stairs,\n26\t  platforms in the air, pits, pillars, braziers and marks, each character's meaning and look in a legend. The ground is\n27\t  baked once into one texture (contact shadows at the foot of walls), the sides of raised floors merged per look, walls\n28\t  and pillars instanced. The same text gives Rapier's colliders, the heights the game asks about (`ground`, `floorAt`,\n29\t  `topH`) and a walkable grid with flow fields that climb stairs and drop off ledges.\n30\t- **Actors**: a hero is a kinematic capsule walked by Rapier's character controller (stairs, slopes, curbs, jumps,\n31\t  moving platforms, pushing crates, shoving mobs); a mob is a dynamic body, so a crowd pushes and piles up by itself and\n32\t  knockback is momentum. Each step the game sets what it wants (a ground velocity, a jump, its rig's move) and\n33\t  `world.step` moves it and poses its rig from the 2D engine's rigs and moves (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.Attack`,\n34\t  `E.Combo`, `E.MOVES`).\n35\t- **Characters** are drawn as the 3D Prototype drew them: cards (the 2D engine draws each rig from the camera's own turn\n36\t  and tilt onto a card in an atlas) or puppets (3D parts on the same joints, in instanced batches with outlines).\n37\t- **Things**: pickups and set dressing drawn by code from four shapes with a pen (`world.thing('coin', ...)`, chests,\n38\t  doors, flags, springs, trees...), and a registry a game adds kinds to.\n39\t- **Shapes**: the 2D engine's objects as data (`docs/ASSETS.md`: the core library, the store's sets, a game's own\n40\t  entries) drawn in 3D from the same resolved parts (`E.shapeMesh`), each material's pattern a tile of one atlas texture\n41\t  (`E.materialTile`) on one toon material, so a new shape builds no shader; a solid one gets a fixed collider from the\n42\t  box the 2D engine collides with (`E.shapeBox`). `world.shape(id, x, y, z, o)`, or `{ shape: 'bench' }` in a legend.\n43\t- **The camera**: the 2D engine's views as real 3D cameras (`game.view`, `game.yaw`, `game.zoom`: V, [ ], the wheel, a\n44\t  pinch and Settings work as in 2D), a chase camera that swings round behind the hero, first person, fly and fixed (in those a click captures the mouse to\n45\t  turn the camera; not while a session is recorded, whose record has no pointer lock). Its\n46\t  pose is worked out in the fixed steps, so the controls that read it replay the same.\n47\t- **Camera zones, lock-on, tank controls** (v0.79.0): `camera.zones` and the `'zones'` mode give each region of a level\n48\t  its own camera (fixed, one that turns after the target, one that slides on a rail), Resident Evil's: the cut waits\n49\t  until the target is `margin` units into the next region, so a border never flicks back and forth, and across a cut\n50\t  `camera.ground` keeps the old camera's way while the stick is held the same way. `camera.lock(hero, mobs)` locks on\n51\t  (Zelda's targeting): the one in sight (a clear line, in range) nearest where the hero faces; `cycle` goes round to the\n52\t  next; the chase and first cameras swing behind the hero to frame both and `ground()` strafes round the target; a\n53\t  reticle marks it; it lets go of a target that dies, leaves or gets too far. Whom it picks reads only the world, never\n54\t  the camera, so it replays the same. `camera.steer` is camera-relative or tank controls, by the player's setting\n55\t  (`E.World3D.CONTROLS`).\n56\t- **Doors between rooms** (v0.79.0): a door thing swings on hinges (`swing: true`) or rises, takes a key\n57\t  (`locked: 'iron'`, `world.unlock(door, inv)`), and `world.through(door, hero)` plays Resident Evil's door opening:\n58\t  the camera cuts to the door, it swings open as the camera goes through, the hero comes out beyond it, while the rest\n59\t  of the world holds still (`world.passing`). `world.reach(hero)` is the thing in front within reach: what a check\n60\t  button acts on. `trunk` (an item box) and `typewriter` (a save point, with the 2D engine's `game.saves`) are things.\n61\t- **Vehicles and tracks** (v0.80.0): `world.vehicle({ kind: 'kart' | 'car' | 'hover' })` is a ball Rapier rolls (walls,\n62\t  bumps, ramps and jumps come from the physics) steered by the drive module's arcade handling, E.Car's: speed along\n63\t  the heading, a slide that grip takes away, a kart's hop into a drift that charges a boost, the ground's surface under\n64\t  it (a track's road, its boost pads, the grass beside it, the level's ground) slowing or boosting it; it is drawn by\n65\t  code with the pen on its tilt (the ground's slope under it), its driver seated. E.Race races it as it races an E.Car\n66\t  (`race.update(dt, null)`: Rapier does the bumps). Rapier's ray-cast vehicle controller (a chassis on four sprung\n67\t  wheels) was the other way; the Vehicle Lab (Temporary, on the Labs page) drives both on one track, and D62 says why\n68\t  the engine took the ball. `world.track({ path, width, bank, walls, pads, off })` builds the road along an `E.Path`\n69\t  with heights: one mesh (asphalt, a dashed line, striped kerbs), banked at its bends, low walls, solid as a triangle\n70\t  mesh and wall boxes; `track.where(x, y)` says how far along, how far across and the road's height there.\n71\t- **Flight** (v0.80.0): `world.flyer(o)` is arcade flight worked out in the steps, not a body: yaw, pitch, a bank into\n72\t  turns, boost and brake, barrel rolls and loops, free within bounds (it turns back at their edge and keeps above the\n73\t  ground) or on rails (Star Fox: the rail's point rides along a path and the ship moves about a window across it, the\n74\t  camera following the rail's point); `f.seek(list)` finds what a charged shot locks on, in a cone, in range and in\n75\t  sight. The camera's `'behind'` mode swings after a heading and a pitch: a kart's camera and a ship's.\n76\t- **Split screen** (v0.80.0): with the 2D engine's `game.splitScreen(n)`, viewport i draws through `world.cameras[i]`\n77\t  (`world.addCamera(o)`) in its own part of the one 3D canvas (a scissor each; the first clears), the HUD drawn per\n78\t  viewport as in 2D; filters are off while split.\n79\t- **The picture**: torches that light the nearest action (one casting shadows), a sun with shadows, a flashlight that\n80\t  follows an actor's facing and flickers like a failing bulb (`world.flashlight`, a spot light made with the world), a sky, fog,\n81\t  the game's particles in 3D, ground arcs (telegraphs), glows, weapon trails, and the 3D Prototype's filters (Comic cel,\n82\t  Pixel, bloom, FXAA); the player picks cards or puppets, the resolution, shadows and the filter in the pause menu\n83\t  (`E.World3D.SETTINGS`). Every shader is built in a warm-up frame behind the boot screen, so none stalls the game.\n84\t- **Open worlds** (v0.47.0): `terrain: { height, color, ... }` in place of a level. The ground is the game's height\n85\t  function sampled every 2 m into 64 m chunks, each sampled the first time anything asks about it, so a map kilometres\n86\t  across costs only where someone goes. The same samples make the three things the ground is: Rapier heightfields for the\n87\t  chunks near actors (streamed in `world.step`, so a replay streams the same), the queries (`ground`, `floorAt`, `topH`,\n88\t  `terrain.normal`) on exactly the triangles Rapier collides with, and the picture: a quadtree of meshes from one pool,\n89\t  fine near the camera and coarser out to `far`, skirts hiding the seams, the game's colors per vertex and red rock in\n90\t  strata where it steepens, all in one material (streaming never builds a shader). A dome sky follows the camera.\n91\t- **Shooters** (v0.47.0): `world.ray` (hitscan, ledges, feet on the ground: the first thing hit, its normal, the actor or\n92\t  prop), `world.block` (fixed colliders for things a game draws its own way), `actor.resize` (a crouch, a slide), a\n93\t  `'fixed'` camera with roll and a mouse it can capture for the game to turn itself (`camera.takeMouse()`), and the\n94\t  overlay: a scene drawn over the world after its depth is cleared, from its own camera, lit like the world, with its own\n95\t  pen in the eye's frame, so a first-person gun never sinks into a wall. `onDraw` runs the game's own drawing every\n96\t  frame, whichever scene draws the world (a game over over it). `camera.target` sizes the outlines and centres the sun's\n97\t  shadow, so a shooter points it ahead of the eye.\n98\t- **The proof**: `world.hash()` after a scripted run is the same on WebGPU, WebGL 2 and run again: the game never reads\n99\t  the renderer.\n100\t\n101\t## The kits\n102\t\n103\tEach 3D slice is a complete small game in the style of the 2D slices (dense, commented, procedural art), with\n104\t`window.__game.scenes.<slice>.run(n)` for its proof:\n105\t\n106\t- **Action** (`src/starter3d/10-action.js`): the stress test's game cut down to a kit. A torch-lit hall with pillars,\n107\t  low walls and a raised dais; a three-hit combo, a dash and the thrust out of it, a jump, an ember bolt, lock-on (Q); husks,\n108\t  skeletons, slimes and wisps in five waves that take turns to attack (at most three at once) and telegraph their swings,\n109\t  then a knight; big hits throw bodies; crates and barrels break and drop hearts. Bookcases, benches, sacks and an anvil\n110\t  (shapes, in the level's legend) stand along its walls.\n111\t- **Platformer** (`src/starter3d/20-platformer.js`): a sunlit course of grass islands over the void, from the start to\n112\t  the flag: plank platforms, a terrace and a tower of stone steps, a platform that rides across a gap, a spring up to a\n113\t  high deck; a jump held for height, a double jump, a dash on the ground and once in the air; slimes to stomp; coins,\n114\t  three stars, checkpoint flags. Its trees (the 2D engine's tree generator), bushes and rocks are shapes.\n115\t- **Shooter** (`src/starter3d/30-shooter.js`): Apex Legends' movement and gunplay without the multiplayer, in first or\n116\t  third person (T). A badlands two kilometres across on a terrain (golden grass that sways, red mesas in strata, dry\n117\t  riverbeds, junipers and pines streamed by chunk), six outposts on flat pads joined by roads routed round the steep\n118\t  ground (A*), each laid out in metres as one merged mesh over fixed blocks: buildings with doors and windows, containers,\n119\t  watch towers with ladders and ziplines, supply bins. Sprint, crouch, slide (faster downhill), jump, vault, mantle,\n120\t  climb, ladders, ziplines, doors opened or kicked off their hinges; a rifle and a pump shotgun (hip fire and sights,\n121\t  spread and bloom, a learnable recoil pattern, reloads with the mag in the hand), a punch with a lunge, grenades;\n122\t  hitscan with tracers, impacts and holes by surface, muzzle flashes and hits as stars that last two frames, shields\n123\t  before health, headshots, hit markers (shield, health, head, a broken shield, a kill) and damage numbers.\n124\t  Raiders hold five outposts (they spawn as you near them, see and hear, fight in bursts with an aim that lags a fifth of a second, so moving matters, strafe, hunt); the drop point\n125\t  is a firing range with targets that stand up again. Every soldier is drawn with the pen on a procedural skeleton (feet\n126\t  planted by IK on whatever is under them and stepping round as it turns on the spot, the pelvis lowering for long strides, a spine that leans and twists, hands on\n127\t  the gun by IK; slides, vaults, climbs, a death that folds and falls); first person draws its arms and guns with the\n128\t  overlay's pen in layers (stance, sway, bob, kick, reloads, punches, kicks, hands on ledges and rungs).\n129\t\n130\t- **Horror** (`src/starter3d/40-horror.js`): Resident Evil and Alone in the Dark. A mansion's two wings and their\n131\t  rooms (a foyer with a typewriter and an item box, two halls, a kitchen, a dining room, a library, a gallery) under\n132\t  fixed cameras by room (`camera.zones`: fixed, turning, on a rail), lit by braziers and a flashlight with a failing bulb,\n133\t  in fog. A pistol held to aim: it locks on the nearest thing in front (`camera.lock`), aims high (a head) and low (a\n134\t  dog), cycles to the next; a knife; few bullets. Zombies that shamble and grab (tap to shake free), dogs drawn with the\n135\t  pen that run and leap, and a hulking thing in the gallery that swings and charges. Doors between rooms\n136\t  (`world.through`), two keys for two locks, a crank for the grille over the second, herbs that mix (the rpg module's\n137\t  six-slot inventory), an item box, saves at the typewriter (`game.saves`) and an autosave at each door. Tank or modern\n138\t  controls (Settings). `HORROR.bot()` plays it from the foyer through every room to the boss and out.\n139\t- **Racing** (`src/starter3d/50-racing.js`, the drive module): Mario Kart 64 and Crash Team Racing. A kart circuit in\n140\t  a valley (`world.track`: banked bends, a hill whose crest throws you, a jump over a stream, boost pads; its walls\n141\t  opened where a dirt shortcut, a track of its own without walls, crosses the infield's hairpin), item boxes in rows,\n142\t  you and five drivers (`world.vehicle` karts with their rigs seated, raced by the drive module's `E.Race`: laps\n143\t  through checkpoints, places, its drivers, a boost, a shell and a peel), three laps; a kart off the course a while is\n144\t  set back on the road (Lakitu). VS: two players in a split screen (`game.splitScreen`, `world.addCamera`). The title\n145\t  races the drivers by themselves. `RACING.bot()` races every kart to the end (yours over the shortcut each lap), then\n146\t  two players split.\n147\t- **Flight** (`src/starter3d/60-flight.js`): Star Fox 64 and Panzer Dragoon. A terrain of sea, rock islands, a coast,\n148\t  the enemy's base on a plateau and mountains round a bay. The stage on rails (`world.flyer` with `rail`: the camera\n149\t  behind the rail's point, the ship moving in a window across it): fighters in waves on the rail's frame, rings that\n150\t  mend the shield, turrets and masts on the base, a fighter on the wingman's tail to shoot off it; then free flight in\n151\t  the bay (`bounds`) round a flagship (four engines, then its core). Twin lasers, a charged shot that locks on\n152\t  (`f.seek`), barrel rolls that turn shots aside, boost and brake on a meter, KESTREL on the radio (`Dialog`, not\n153\t  modal). `FLIGHT.bot()` flies the stage and downs the flagship.\n154\t- **Crawler** (`src/starter3d/70-crawler.js`): Eye of the Beholder, Dungeon Master and Wizardry. The Lantern Vaults, a\n155\t  map of cells (each 2 x 2 of the level's tiles: corridors two metres wide) with braziers in niches, a ceiling drawn over\n156\t  it and the party's lantern. The camera is `'fixed'` at the party's eyes, its pose tweened per step (steps, quarter\n157\t  turns, a step aside, a lurch into a wall). A party of four (`rig.drawPortrait` on its cards): two in front who\n158\t  strike what stands before them, a cleric who heals, a mage whose bolt flies down the corridor. Gates that rise\n159\t  (`world.thing('door')`, one with an iron lock: `world.unlock`), a lever on a wall, a plate that raises a gate and one\n160\t  that springs darts, wisps, slimes, the dead and the Warden walked cell to cell on the grid module's paths, an automap\n161\t  from `E.Fog`; real time (`E.Turns` `'atb'`: each one's gauge) or turns (`'speed'`: the vaults wait). `CRAWLER.bot()`\n162\t  plays to the Warden and up the stair, then checks that in turns the vaults hold still until the party moves.\n163\t\n164\tA kit is the minified 2D engine, the API card, the minified 3D engine with its manual, the shell and one slice: about\n165\t200,000 cl100k tokens (a 2D kit is about 180,000 on the same count), so a 200k-token chat holds it with little room; a\n166\tcoding agent reads the 3D engine's header and the API card and builds from those. Unlike a 2D kit, a 3D kit is not one\n167\tself-contained file: it loads the two vendored libraries through its import map, so it is served (D27).\n168\t\n169\t## Checks\n170\t\n171\t`node tools/kit3d-test.mjs`: the 3D labs' rules on the 3D engine's and the kits' sources (WebGPURenderer and node\n172\tmaterials only, no compute or storage buffers, nothing read back from the GPU, vendored imports); the manual's quick start\n173\trun as the manual hosts it; every 3D page on WebGL 2 and WebGPU (SwiftShader, through the stand-in canvas the 3D labs'\n174\ttests use): the title draws, each slice starts, its proof gives the same hash twice and on both backends, a few seconds\n175\tof scripted play move the hero and build no GPU pipeline mid-game; the camera's contracts (lock-on, zones and their\n176\tcuts, the stick across a cut, tank steering, the door between rooms) at four refresh rates' steps; vehicles, tracks,\n177\tflight and split screen on both backends (top speed, a boost pad, a wall, the grass, a drift's boost, a hovercraft's\n178\tcushion, a banked bend, E.Race racing two AI vehicles a lap, rails, a barrel roll, bounds, `seek`; twice the same and\n179\tthe same on each; two viewports drawn); every core shape built and drawn on both backends,\n180\tsolid where it stands (a hero stops at a crate, lands on a table), and a sheet of each beside its 2D picture; every kit on an upright phone (the 3D picture lies exactly under\n181\tthe game's, the touch buttons are there). `tools/kits-test.mjs` plays each 3D slice's bot (the horror wing to its end, a race of six karts and a split race, the flight to the flagship's end, the vaults to the Warden and out). Pictures in `check-output/kit3d/`. `tools/doctrine-check.mjs` holds the manual\n182\tto its budget; `tools/version.mjs` keeps its version line with the rest.\n183\t\n184\t## Things to know\n185\t\n186\t- **three r182's `compileAsync`** left a light's shadow without its map in this setup (an error on the next frame), so\n187\t  `world.prepare()` draws a warm-up frame instead.\n188\t- **Rapier's character controller follows a moving floor on some steps and not others.** `world.step` carries a hero on\n189\t  a moving platform by the platform's own step, unless the controller has already added it that step.\n190\t- **Point lights' shadows are expensive**: each renders the scene six times. A world makes one torch shadow by default\n191\t  (`torchShadows`), the 3D Prototype two; a sun's shadow is one pass.\n192\t- **Nested TSL `select()`** chains rendered a material black on WebGL 2 here (each branch alone was right); a sum of\n193\t  weights (1 for the matching kind, 0 for the rest) picks a pattern instead.\n194\t- **`world.pen` and `overlay.pen` hold what a frame drew until it rendered** (they empty after the render, not before it:\n195\t  until v0.47.0 a game's own pen drawing was wiped by `world.draw` itself).\n196\t- **An `InstancedMesh` of more than 1,000 instances** never uses `DynamicDrawUsage` (three r182 drops its updates); with\n197\t  1,000 or fewer each such mesh's shader is its own, so the card atlas pages keep room for 1,001.\n198\t- **Such a mesh's matrices upload in the ranges of the frame before** (three r182 uploads a render object's geometry,\n199\t  then updates its nodes, which copy the mesh's update ranges), so a slot past the last frame's count drew, for one\n200\t  frame, whatever it last held: zeros, or a part from frames ago. On the overlay, whose camera is the origin, a zero\n201\t  matrix put an outline's corners at the eye and filled the screen with ink on the first shot (until v0.48.0). So the pen's\n202\t  batches and the card pages upload up to the most slots ever drawn and some room past them, and a slot never drawn holds\n203\t  a part a hundred kilometres away and a millimetre across; a part of no width (no normals: a NaN outline) isn't drawn.\n204\t- **A world drew its last step as it was** (until v0.51.0). The steps run 120 times a second, so a 144 Hz display showed\n205\t  one frame in six again (no step ran) and a 90 Hz one alternated one and two steps a frame: every still was right and\n206\t  the motion juddered (measured at 144 Hz: the camera's move from frame to frame varied by 45% in the shooter, the\n207\t  platformer and the action kit). Now `world.draw` puts the actors and the camera part way between the last two steps\n208\t  (`game.alpha`), and a pause or a hit-stop shows the last step as it is; a camera pose the game sets itself in `onDraw`\n209\t  is the game's to place, and what a game draws with the pen is drawn where its own numbers say: the shooter draws its\n210\t  soldiers and camera part way itself (`keepPrev`, `between`, the frame's clock `drawT`).\n211\t- **A track's triangles face up in engine axes, not three's** (z is up in the engine, y in three): wound the other way,\n212\t  Rapier pushed a vehicle down through the road and three culled the road from above. A road laid flat on a flat floor\n213\t  fought it for the same pixels, so a track sits 1 unit up (`lift`).\n214\t- **Rapier's ray-cast vehicle controller felt its own chassis** until its query left out dynamic bodies and the vehicle's\n215\t  own group (the Vehicle Lab's kart A sank and would not move).\n216\t- The 3D Prototype (`/stress-world`, once Stress test: 3D world) stayed on the Labs page as the record of what the\n217\t  engine was built from (the owner's call, 2026-10-14) until v0.51.4, when it was deleted with the 3D Proof (D44): its\n218\t  code is in git, its record in `docs/LAB-3D.md`, and its addresses lead to `/3d`. New 3D work goes into the engine and\n219\t  its kits.\n220\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800","uuid":"b59af193-1c19-4ccd-b4f1-0dbf4e6fa51a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:53:07.907Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.cache/crackmania-plan-research-xbd1c73/engine/docs/ENGINE-3D.md","content":"# The 3D engine and the 3D kits\n\n`engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (v0.34.0) puts a real 3D world under a my-3D2dge game: a level written as text (or an open world's terrain), its physics,\nits characters, its camera and its picture, drawn by three.js r182 and simulated by Rapier 0.19.3. It is the stress\ntest's 3D world (the 3D Prototype, deleted in v0.51.4; `docs/LAB-3D.md`) taken apart and made general, so that a game can be built on it the\nway the 2D starter kits are built on the engine. Its header is its manual: an agent reads that and the API card, never\nthe code below them.\n\n| What | Where |\n| --- | --- |\n| The 3D engine (an ES module) | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`; built copy `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` |\n| Its manual | its header (the `/*! ... */` comment), with a complete quick-start game; a budget of 8,000 tokens (D27, D60) |\n| The 3D starter: a title stage and every 3D slice | `src/starter3d/` and `src/starter3d.template.html`, built into `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.html` ([/3d](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d)) |\n| The 3D kits, one slice each | `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-action.html` ([/kits/3d-action](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/3d-action)), `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-platformer.html` ([/kits/3d-platformer](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/3d-platformer)), `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-shooter.html` ([/kits/3d-shooter](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/3d-shooter)), `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-horror.html` ([/kits/3d-horror](https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/3d-horror)) |\n| The check | `node tools/kit3d-test.mjs` (in `npm test`) |\n| The libraries | `vendor/three-0.182.0/`, `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` (pinned, with checksums: `node tools/vendor-3d.mjs --check`) |\n\n## How it fits the 2D engine\n\nThe 2D engine still runs the game. `new E.Game({ ..., bg: null })` makes its canvas clear (new in v0.34.0), so the\ngame's HUD, menus, notes and touch buttons draw over the 3D picture; everything a 2D game gets comes with it: the loop of\nexact fixed steps, scenes, input with rebinding and touch buttons, the pause menu and settings, full screen, the boot\nscreen, sound. The 3D engine adds `E.World3D` (and `E.THREE`, `E.TSL`, `E.RAPIER`):\n\n- **The level is text**, one character per 1-metre tile, as the stress test's hall was: walls, raised floors, stairs,\n  platforms in the air, pits, pillars, braziers and marks, each character's meaning and look in a legend. The ground is\n  baked once into one texture (contact shadows at the foot of walls), the sides of raised floors merged per look, walls\n  and pillars instanced. The same text gives Rapier's colliders, the heights the game asks about (`ground`, `floorAt`,\n  `topH`) and a walkable grid with flow fields that climb stairs and drop off ledges.\n- **Actors**: a hero is a kinematic capsule walked by Rapier's character controller (stairs, slopes, curbs, jumps,\n  moving platforms, pushing crates, shoving mobs); a mob is a dynamic body, so a crowd pushes and piles up by itself and\n  knockback is momentum. Each step the game sets what it wants (a ground velocity, a jump, its rig's move) and\n  `world.step` moves it and poses its rig from the 2D engine's rigs and moves (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.Attack`,\n  `E.Combo`, `E.MOVES`).\n- **Characters** are drawn as the 3D Prototype drew them: cards (the 2D engine draws each rig from the camera's own turn\n  and tilt onto a card in an atlas) or puppets (3D parts on the same joints, in instanced batches with outlines).\n- **Things**: pickups and set dressing drawn by code from four shapes with a pen (`world.thing('coin', ...)`, chests,\n  doors, flags, springs, trees...), and a registry a game adds kinds to.\n- **Shapes**: the 2D engine's objects as data (`docs/ASSETS.md`: the core library, the store's sets, a game's own\n  entries) drawn in 3D from the same resolved parts (`E.shapeMesh`), each material's pattern a tile of one atlas texture\n  (`E.materialTile`) on one toon material, so a new shape builds no shader; a solid one gets a fixed collider from the\n  box the 2D engine collides with (`E.shapeBox`). `world.shape(id, x, y, z, o)`, or `{ shape: 'bench' }` in a legend.\n- **The camera**: the 2D engine's views as real 3D cameras (`game.view`, `game.yaw`, `game.zoom`: V, [ ], the wheel, a\n  pinch and Settings work as in 2D), a chase camera that swings round behind the hero, first person, fly and fixed (in those a click captures the mouse to\n  turn the camera; not while a session is recorded, whose record has no pointer lock). Its\n  pose is worked out in the fixed steps, so the controls that read it replay the same.\n- **Camera zones, lock-on, tank controls** (v0.79.0): `camera.zones` and the `'zones'` mode give each region of a level\n  its own camera (fixed, one that turns after the target, one that slides on a rail), Resident Evil's: the cut waits\n  until the target is `margin` units into the next region, so a border never flicks back and forth, and across a cut\n  `camera.ground` keeps the old camera's way while the stick is held the same way. `camera.lock(hero, mobs)` locks on\n  (Zelda's targeting): the one in sight (a clear line, in range) nearest where the hero faces; `cycle` goes round to the\n  next; the chase and first cameras swing behind the hero to frame both and `ground()` strafes round the target; a\n  reticle marks it; it lets go of a target that dies, leaves or gets too far. Whom it picks reads only the world, never\n  the camera, so it replays the same. `camera.steer` is camera-relative or tank controls, by the player's setting\n  (`E.World3D.CONTROLS`).\n- **Doors between rooms** (v0.79.0): a door thing swings on hinges (`swing: true`) or rises, takes a key\n  (`locked: 'iron'`, `world.unlock(door, inv)`), and `world.through(door, hero)` plays Resident Evil's door opening:\n  the camera cuts to the door, it swings open as the camera goes through, the hero comes out beyond it, while the rest\n  of the world holds still (`world.passing`). `world.reach(hero)` is the thing in front within reach: what a check\n  button acts on. `trunk` (an item box) and `typewriter` (a save point, with the 2D engine's `game.saves`) are things.\n- **Vehicles and tracks** (v0.80.0): `world.vehicle({ kind: 'kart' | 'car' | 'hover' })` is a ball Rapier rolls (walls,\n  bumps, ramps and jumps come from the physics) steered by the drive module's arcade handling, E.Car's: speed along\n  the heading, a slide that grip takes away, a kart's hop into a drift that charges a boost, the ground's surface under\n  it (a track's road, its boost pads, the grass beside it, the level's ground) slowing or boosting it; it is drawn by\n  code with the pen on its tilt (the ground's slope under it), its driver seated. E.Race races it as it races an E.Car\n  (`race.update(dt, null)`: Rapier does the bumps). Rapier's ray-cast vehicle controller (a chassis on four sprung\n  wheels) was the other way; the Vehicle Lab (Temporary, on the Labs page) drives both on one track, and D62 says why\n  the engine took the ball. `world.track({ path, width, bank, walls, pads, off })` builds the road along an `E.Path`\n  with heights: one mesh (asphalt, a dashed line, striped kerbs), banked at its bends, low walls, solid as a triangle\n  mesh and wall boxes; `track.where(x, y)` says how far along, how far across and the road's height there.\n- **Flight** (v0.80.0): `world.flyer(o)` is arcade flight worked out in the steps, not a body: yaw, pitch, a bank into\n  turns, boost and brake, barrel rolls and loops, free within bounds (it turns back at their edge and keeps above the\n  ground) or on rails (Star Fox: the rail's point rides along a path and the ship moves about a window across it, the\n  camera following the rail's point); `f.seek(list)` finds what a charged shot locks on, in a cone, in range and in\n  sight. The camera's `'behind'` mode swings after a heading and a pitch: a kart's camera and a ship's.\n- **Split screen** (v0.80.0): with the 2D engine's `game.splitScreen(n)`, viewport i draws through `world.cameras[i]`\n  (`world.addCamera(o)`) in its own part of the one 3D canvas (a scissor each; the first clears), the HUD drawn per\n  viewport as in 2D; filters are off while split.\n- **The picture**: torches that light the nearest action (one casting shadows), a sun with shadows, a flashlight that\n  follows an actor's facing and flickers like a failing bulb (`world.flashlight`, a spot light made with the world), a sky, fog,\n  the game's particles in 3D, ground arcs (telegraphs), glows, weapon trails, and the 3D Prototype's filters (Comic cel,\n  Pixel, bloom, FXAA); the player picks cards or puppets, the resolution, shadows and the filter in the pause menu\n  (`E.World3D.SETTINGS`). Every shader is built in a warm-up frame behind the boot screen, so none stalls the game.\n- **Open worlds** (v0.47.0): `terrain: { height, color, ... }` in place of a level. The ground is the game's height\n  function sampled every 2 m into 64 m chunks, each sampled the first time anything asks about it, so a map kilometres\n  across costs only where someone goes. The same samples make the three things the ground is: Rapier heightfields for the\n  chunks near actors (streamed in `world.step`, so a replay streams the same), the queries (`ground`, `floorAt`, `topH`,\n  `terrain.normal`) on exactly the triangles Rapier collides with, and the picture: a quadtree of meshes from one pool,\n  fine near the camera and coarser out to `far`, skirts hiding the seams, the game's colors per vertex and red rock in\n  strata where it steepens, all in one material (streaming never builds a shader). A dome sky follows the camera.\n- **Shooters** (v0.47.0): `world.ray` (hitscan, ledges, feet on the ground: the first thing hit, its normal, the actor or\n  prop), `world.block` (fixed colliders for things a game draws its own way), `actor.resize` (a crouch, a slide), a\n  `'fixed'` camera with roll and a mouse it can capture for the game to turn itself (`camera.takeMouse()`), and the\n  overlay: a scene drawn over the world after its depth is cleared, from its own camera, lit like the world, with its own\n  pen in the eye's frame, so a first-person gun never sinks into a wall. `onDraw` runs the game's own drawing every\n  frame, whichever scene draws the world (a game over over it). `camera.target` sizes the outlines and centres the sun's\n  shadow, so a shooter points it ahead of the eye.\n- **The proof**: `world.hash()` after a scripted run is the same on WebGPU, WebGL 2 and run again: the game never reads\n  the renderer.\n\n## The kits\n\nEach 3D slice is a complete small game in the style of the 2D slices (dense, commented, procedural art), with\n`window.__game.scenes.<slice>.run(n)` for its proof:\n\n- **Action** (`src/starter3d/10-action.js`): the stress test's game cut down to a kit. A torch-lit hall with pillars,\n  low walls and a raised dais; a three-hit combo, a dash and the thrust out of it, a jump, an ember bolt, lock-on (Q); husks,\n  skeletons, slimes and wisps in five waves that take turns to attack (at most three at once) and telegraph their swings,\n  then a knight; big hits throw bodies; crates and barrels break and drop hearts. Bookcases, benches, sacks and an anvil\n  (shapes, in the level's legend) stand along its walls.\n- **Platformer** (`src/starter3d/20-platformer.js`): a sunlit course of grass islands over the void, from the start to\n  the flag: plank platforms, a terrace and a tower of stone steps, a platform that rides across a gap, a spring up to a\n  high deck; a jump held for height, a double jump, a dash on the ground and once in the air; slimes to stomp; coins,\n  three stars, checkpoint flags. Its trees (the 2D engine's tree generator), bushes and rocks are shapes.\n- **Shooter** (`src/starter3d/30-shooter.js`): Apex Legends' movement and gunplay without the multiplayer, in first or\n  third person (T). A badlands two kilometres across on a terrain (golden grass that sways, red mesas in strata, dry\n  riverbeds, junipers and pines streamed by chunk), six outposts on flat pads joined by roads routed round the steep\n  ground (A*), each laid out in metres as one merged mesh over fixed blocks: buildings with doors and windows, containers,\n  watch towers with ladders and ziplines, supply bins. Sprint, crouch, slide (faster downhill), jump, vault, mantle,\n  climb, ladders, ziplines, doors opened or kicked off their hinges; a rifle and a pump shotgun (hip fire and sights,\n  spread and bloom, a learnable recoil pattern, reloads with the mag in the hand), a punch with a lunge, grenades;\n  hitscan with tracers, impacts and holes by surface, muzzle flashes and hits as stars that last two frames, shields\n  before health, headshots, hit markers (shield, health, head, a broken shield, a kill) and damage numbers.\n  Raiders hold five outposts (they spawn as you near them, see and hear, fight in bursts with an aim that lags a fifth of a second, so moving matters, strafe, hunt); the drop point\n  is a firing range with targets that stand up again. Every soldier is drawn with the pen on a procedural skeleton (feet\n  planted by IK on whatever is under them and stepping round as it turns on the spot, the pelvis lowering for long strides, a spine that leans and twists, hands on\n  the gun by IK; slides, vaults, climbs, a death that folds and falls); first person draws its arms and guns with the\n  overlay's pen in layers (stance, sway, bob, kick, reloads, punches, kicks, hands on ledges and rungs).\n\n- **Horror** (`src/starter3d/40-horror.js`): Resident Evil and Alone in the Dark. A mansion's two wings and their\n  rooms (a foyer with a typewriter and an item box, two halls, a kitchen, a dining room, a library, a gallery) under\n  fixed cameras by room (`camera.zones`: fixed, turning, on a rail), lit by braziers and a flashlight with a failing bulb,\n  in fog. A pistol held to aim: it locks on the nearest thing in front (`camera.lock`), aims high (a head) and low (a\n  dog), cycles to the next; a knife; few bullets. Zombies that shamble and grab (tap to shake free), dogs drawn with the\n  pen that run and leap, and a hulking thing in the gallery that swings and charges. Doors between rooms\n  (`world.through`), two keys for two locks, a crank for the grille over the second, herbs that mix (the rpg module's\n  six-slot inventory), an item box, saves at the typewriter (`game.saves`) and an autosave at each door. Tank or modern\n  controls (Settings). `HORROR.bot()` plays it from the foyer through every room to the boss and out.\n- **Racing** (`src/starter3d/50-racing.js`, the drive module): Mario Kart 64 and Crash Team Racing. A kart circuit in\n  a valley (`world.track`: banked bends, a hill whose crest throws you, a jump over a stream, boost pads; its walls\n  opened where a dirt shortcut, a track of its own without walls, crosses the infield's hairpin), item boxes in rows,\n  you and five drivers (`world.vehicle` karts with their rigs seated, raced by the drive module's `E.Race`: laps\n  through checkpoints, places, its drivers, a boost, a shell and a peel), three laps; a kart off the course a while is\n  set back on the road (Lakitu). VS: two players in a split screen (`game.splitScreen`, `world.addCamera`). The title\n  races the drivers by themselves. `RACING.bot()` races every kart to the end (yours over the shortcut each lap), then\n  two players split.\n- **Flight** (`src/starter3d/60-flight.js`): Star Fox 64 and Panzer Dragoon. A terrain of sea, rock islands, a coast,\n  the enemy's base on a plateau and mountains round a bay. The stage on rails (`world.flyer` with `rail`: the camera\n  behind the rail's point, the ship moving in a window across it): fighters in waves on the rail's frame, rings that\n  mend the shield, turrets and masts on the base, a fighter on the wingman's tail to shoot off it; then free flight in\n  the bay (`bounds`) round a flagship (four engines, then its core). Twin lasers, a charged shot that locks on\n  (`f.seek`), barrel rolls that turn shots aside, boost and brake on a meter, KESTREL on the radio (`Dialog`, not\n  modal). `FLIGHT.bot()` flies the stage and downs the flagship.\n- **Crawler** (`src/starter3d/70-crawler.js`): Eye of the Beholder, Dungeon Master and Wizardry. The Lantern Vaults, a\n  map of cells (each 2 x 2 of the level's tiles: corridors two metres wide) with braziers in niches, a ceiling drawn over\n  it and the party's lantern. The camera is `'fixed'` at the party's eyes, its pose tweened per step (steps, quarter\n  turns, a step aside, a lurch into a wall). A party of four (`rig.drawPortrait` on its cards): two in front who\n  strike what stands before them, a cleric who heals, a mage whose bolt flies down the corridor. Gates that rise\n  (`world.thing('door')`, one with an iron lock: `world.unlock`), a lever on a wall, a plate that raises a gate and one\n  that springs darts, wisps, slimes, the dead and the Warden walked cell to cell on the grid module's paths, an automap\n  from `E.Fog`; real time (`E.Turns` `'atb'`: each one's gauge) or turns (`'speed'`: the vaults wait). `CRAWLER.bot()`\n  plays to the Warden and up the stair, then checks that in turns the vaults hold still until the party moves.\n\nA kit is the minified 2D engine, the API card, the minified 3D engine with its manual, the shell and one slice: about\n200,000 cl100k tokens (a 2D kit is about 180,000 on the same count), so a 200k-token chat holds it with little room; a\ncoding agent reads the 3D engine's header and the API card and builds from those. Unlike a 2D kit, a 3D kit is not one\nself-contained file: it loads the two vendored libraries through its import map, so it is served (D27).\n\n## Checks\n\n`node tools/kit3d-test.mjs`: the 3D labs' rules on the 3D engine's and the kits' sources (WebGPURenderer and node\nmaterials only, no compute or storage buffers, nothing read back from the GPU, vendored imports); the manual's quick start\nrun as the manual hosts it; every 3D page on WebGL 2 and WebGPU (SwiftShader, through the stand-in canvas the 3D labs'\ntests use): the title draws, each slice starts, its proof gives the same hash twice and on both backends, a few seconds\nof scripted play move the hero and build no GPU pipeline mid-game; the camera's contracts (lock-on, zones and their\ncuts, the stick across a cut, tank steering, the door between rooms) at four refresh rates' steps; vehicles, tracks,\nflight and split screen on both backends (top speed, a boost pad, a wall, the grass, a drift's boost, a hovercraft's\ncushion, a banked bend, E.Race racing two AI vehicles a lap, rails, a barrel roll, bounds, `seek`; twice the same and\nthe same on each; two viewports drawn); every core shape built and drawn on both backends,\nsolid where it stands (a hero stops at a crate, lands on a table), and a sheet of each beside its 2D picture; every kit on an upright phone (the 3D picture lies exactly under\nthe game's, the touch buttons are there). `tools/kits-test.mjs` plays each 3D slice's bot (the horror wing to its end, a race of six karts and a split race, the flight to the flagship's end, the vaults to the Warden and out). Pictures in `check-output/kit3d/`. `tools/doctrine-check.mjs` holds the manual\nto its budget; `tools/version.mjs` keeps its version line with the rest.\n\n## Things to know\n\n- **three r182's `compileAsync`** left a light's shadow without its map in this setup (an error on the next frame), so\n  `world.prepare()` draws a warm-up frame instead.\n- **Rapier's character controller follows a moving floor on some steps and not others.** `world.step` carries a hero on\n  a moving platform by the platform's own step, unless the controller has already added it that step.\n- **Point lights' shadows are expensive**: each renders the scene six times. A world makes one torch shadow by default\n  (`torchShadows`), the 3D Prototype two; a sun's shadow is one pass.\n- **Nested TSL `select()`** chains rendered a material black on WebGL 2 here (each branch alone was right); a sum of\n  weights (1 for the matching kind, 0 for the rest) picks a pattern instead.\n- **`world.pen` and `overlay.pen` hold what a frame drew until it rendered** (they empty after the render, not before it:\n  until v0.47.0 a game's own pen drawing was wiped by `world.draw` itself).\n- **An `InstancedMesh` of more than 1,000 instances** never uses `DynamicDrawUsage` (three r182 drops its updates); with\n  1,000 or fewer each such mesh's shader is its own, so the card atlas pages keep room for 1,001.\n- **Such a mesh's matrices upload in the ranges of the frame before** (three r182 uploads a render object's geometry,\n  then updates its nodes, which copy the mesh's update ranges), so a slot past the last frame's count drew, for one\n  frame, whatever it last held: zeros, or a part from frames ago. On the overlay, whose camera is the origin, a zero\n  matrix put an outline's corners at the eye and filled the screen with ink on the first shot (until v0.48.0). So the pen's\n  batches and the card pages upload up to the most slots ever drawn and some room past them, and a slot never drawn holds\n  a part a hundred kilometres away and a millimetre across; a part of no width (no normals: a NaN outline) isn't drawn.\n- **A world drew its last step as it was** (until v0.51.0). The steps run 120 times a second, so a 144 Hz display showed\n  one frame in six again (no step ran) and a 90 Hz one alternated one and two steps a frame: every still was right and\n  the motion juddered (measured at 144 Hz: the camera's move from frame to frame varied by 45% in the shooter, the\n  platformer and the action kit). Now `world.draw` puts the actors and the camera part way between the last two steps\n  (`game.alpha`), and a pause or a hit-stop shows the last step as it is; a camera pose the game sets itself in `onDraw`\n  is the game's to place, and what a game draws with the pen is drawn where its own numbers say: the shooter draws its\n  soldiers and camera part way itself (`keepPrev`, `between`, the frame's clock `drawT`).\n- **A track's triangles face up in engine axes, not three's** (z is up in the engine, y in three): wound the other way,\n  Rapier pushed a vehicle down through the road and three culled the road from above. A road laid flat on a flat floor\n  fought it for the same pixels, so a track sits 1 unit up (`lift`).\n- **Rapier's ray-cast vehicle controller felt its own chassis** until its query left out dynamic bodies and the vehicle's\n  own group (the Vehicle Lab's kart A sank and would not move).\n- The 3D Prototype (`/stress-world`, once Stress test: 3D world) stayed on the Labs page as the record of what the\n  engine was built from (the owner's call, 2026-10-14) until v0.51.4, when it was deleted with the 3D Proof (D44): its\n  code is in git, its record in `docs/LAB-3D.md`, and its addresses lead to `/3d`. New 3D work goes into the engine and\n  its kits.\n","numLines":220,"startLine":1,"totalLines":220}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01P5rw2MG8PtbagEGecpYSn4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
