{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WQkzQGpCAJ3vnhomwa1mxm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# CRACKMANIA — build plan\n2\t\n3\t**Title: CRACKMANIA** (\"crack the clock\"). Picked as TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"TrackMania\" were brainstormed, then\n4\tone was drawn with an OS-entropy RNG (`python3 -c \"import secrets; secrets.randbelow(50)\"` → index 8 → *Crackmania*).\n5\tThe name works three ways and the game leans on all of them: a *crack* driver (an expert), *cracking* a track's\n6\tclock, and the puzzle mode's *cracks* (gaps in a track you fill with blocks). The finale track is called \"The Big\n7\tCrack\" (a jump over a canyon).\n8\t\n9\tThis plan is for the developers (possibly other AI models) who will build the game from TASK.md and this file alone.\n10\tRead sections 0–3 whole before writing code; sections 4–9 are the reference you build each system from.\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## 0. Deliverables, names and ground rules\n15\t\n16\t| What | Exact name / place |\n17\t| --- | --- |\n18\t| Game title (title screen, About, hub tags) | `CRACKMANIA` (display), `Crackmania` (file-safe) |\n19\t| SOURCE token in file names | `TrackMania2003` |\n20\t| Dev folder (all source, tools, tests) | `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810/` in the folder that holds TASK.md and PLAN.md |\n21\t| The single-file game | `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810.html` in that same root folder (beside TASK.md) |\n22\t| Exit interview | `exit-interview.json` in the root, beside the HTML (shape in TASK.md; must parse as JSON) |\n23\t| Engine source | `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge`, commit `4390a98` (v0.85.2): `dist/handoff/` (2D engine, docs, tools) **plus** `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` and `vendor/` (the 3D layer; see §2.2) |\n24\t| AI Hub client | `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub`, `lib/hub/` at commit `f8835e4` (protocol 1, client 1.4.0) |\n25\t\n26\tGround rules from TASK.md that shape every decision:\n27\t- Everything is drawn and synthesized by code (no image or sound files). Voices are Animalese-style gibberish.\n28\t- No TODOs, placeholders or stubs in the shipped game. Every feature listed here ships working.\n29\t- Other agents share this machine: work only inside the dev folder (plus a scratch dir of your own), never kill\n30\t  processes you did not start, bind servers to `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind\n31\t  127.0.0.1`), and stop them by PID. During planning `/tmp` (a 9.8 GB tmpfs) was **full**; put clones, npm caches and\n32\t  build output on the main disk (e.g. `npm_config_cache=<devfolder>/.npm-cache`, `TMPDIR=<devfolder>/.tmp`).\n33\t- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§9). Screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n34\t- Final chat reply and the in-game About/Credits screen carry Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and\n35\t  Prompt Feedback (TASK.md lists the fields; \"Plan\" = `PLAN.md`). Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n36\t\n37\t---\n38\t\n39\t## 1. The game in one page\n40\t\n41\t**Pitch.** An original arcade time-attack stunt racer in the spirit of TrackMania (Nadeo, PC, 21 Nov 2003). Short\n42\ttracks built from blocks (loops, kickers, boost pads, banked bends), a clock you race against, a ghost of your best\n43\trun, medals, coppers to spend on track pieces, and an editor where every track — yours or ours — is just blocks on a\n44\tgrid. Three environments with their own car and handling. Original names, art, music, tracks and characters.\n45\t\n46\t**What the source is (research done for this plan).** TrackMania (2003): developer Nadeo; publishers Focus Home\n47\tInteractive (FR), Digital Jesters (UK), Enlight (US, June 2004); director Florent Castelnérac; music Henri-Pierre\n48\tPellegrin. Solo modes **Race, Puzzle and Survival** (Survival — three computer ghost cars, finish last and you're out,\n49\tfirst or second ranks you up — arrived with the Power Up! update), each with tracks in every environment; three\n50\tenvironments **Desert, Rally (countryside) and Snow (alpine)**, each with its own car (muscle \"speed\" cars, rally\n51\tsuper-minis, alpine 4x4s); a **track editor** whose grid-snapped blocks (\"bricks\": roads, checkpoints, long bends,\n52\tloop-the-loops, jumps) are **bought with coppers** earned from bronze/silver/gold medals; a **car painter**;\n53\tmultiplayer by **hot seat, LAN and Internet**; tracks and skins shared online. Metacritic 74. TrackMania's DNA, which\n54\tthis game must capture: *the clock is the enemy*, *instant respawn/restart*, *ghosts and checkpoint splits*, *stunts\n55\tare the route*, *tracks are toys anyone builds*, *terse, playful presentation*.\n56\t\n57\t**Genre:** arcade racing (time attack) + track-building puzzle. **Perspective:** third-person chase camera (far\n58\tchase, close chase, hood cam) in races; an orbiting three-quarter overhead camera over a 3D grid in the editor and\n59\tpuzzles; trackside TV cameras in replays.\n60\t\n61\t**Look:** the engine's native style — low-resolution 3D (the game's 320x240-class buffer, `expand: true`), toon\n62\tshading with ink outlines on code-drawn parts, crisp nearest-filtered pixel textures baked by code, PS1/N64-era\n63\tracer. Bright TrackMania colours: red/white curbs, chunky gates with flip-digit time boards, chevron boost pads.\n64\t\n65\t**Modes (all ship):**\n66\t1. **Race** — 15 tracks (5 per environment), medals Bronze/Silver/Gold/Author, coppers, unlocks.\n67\t2. **Puzzle** — 6 puzzles: start, checkpoints and finish are placed; you get a limited block inventory; build a route,\n68\t   then drive it. Faster routes earn better medals.\n69\t3. **Survival** — per environment, a 5-round gauntlet against three ghost rivals (the \"Ghost Guild\"); last place is\n70\t   out, 1st/2nd rank you up.\n71\t4. **Build** — the track editor (place/rotate/raise/remove blocks, coppers cost, undo/redo, test drive, validate by\n72\t   driving, 6 save slots, share codes).\n73\t5. **Hot Seat** — 2–8 players take turns on one controller/keyboard; standings over a cup of tracks.\n74\t6. **Split Screen** — 2–4 local players at once (pads or keyboard halves); cars are ghosts to each other.\n75\t7. **Online** — AI Hub sessions in the hub's `ghost` mode: everyone time-attacks the same track for 3 minutes and sees\n76\t   the others as ghost cars (TrackMania's own online model).\n77\t8. **Replays** — watch any run with chase/TV/orbit cameras, scrub, save to 10 slots.\n78\t9. **Garage** — paint shop for the three cars (colours, 8 patterns, number, rims).\n79\tPlus: title + attract mode, Dev Mode (pause menu), Settings, About/Credits, touch-only gate.\n80\t\n81\t**Characters (original):** **Coco Asphalte**, a cheerful track architect in a hard hat (host, tutorials, story);\n82\t**Monsieur Drapeau**, the flag man at every start gate; the **Ghost Guild** rivals — **Tex Mirage** (cocky),\n83\t**Mlle Gravillon** (precise), **Bobo Yeti** (chaotic). Cars: **Coyote** (Dustbowl muscle car), **Pebble** (Hedgerow\n84\trally hatch), **Moose** (Frostpeak 4x4). Environments: **Dustbowl** (desert: hot asphalt, big air), **Hedgerow**\n85\t(countryside: tight lanes, loose gravel), **Frostpeak** (alpine: thin air, thin ice).\n86\t\n87\t---\n88\t\n89\t## 2. Technology and architecture\n90\t\n91\t### 2.1 Stack (decided)\n92\t\n93\t| Layer | Choice |\n94\t| --- | --- |\n95\t| Game shell | my-3D2dge **2D engine** v0.85.2 (`dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js`, classic script, every module): fixed 1/120 s steps, scenes, input with rebinding/glyphs, pause menu, settings, boot screen, audio synth, particles, rigs (`E.Humanoid`), pixel font/UI, `E.store`, `game.advance`/`game.debug` for headless tests |\n96\t| 3D | my-3D2dge **3D engine** (`engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` from the repo, ES module, same commit): `E.World3D` (terrain backdrop, sky, fog, sun + shadows, the **pen** for code-drawn parts, particles in 3D, cameras incl. split screen, filters, `world.shape` props, rig actors) on **three.js r182** (WebGPURenderer, falls back to WebGL 2) |\n97\t| Physics queries | **Rapier 0.19.3** (`@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat`, WASM embedded) — our **own** `RAPIER.World` holding the track's fixed colliders; used only for ray casts and shape casts. The car is **not** a Rapier body (§4.4) |\n98\t| Bundling | **esbuild** (dev dependency) bundles three + Rapier + the 3D engine + game modules into one inline `<script type=\"module\">`; the 2D engine is inlined as a classic `<script>` before it; AI Hub `session.js` is the first script in `<head>` |\n99\t| Tools | Node 24 (present), Playwright 1.63.0 (as the handoff pins) + Chromium for browser checks, Python 3 for the hub harness server |\n100\t\n101\tWhy the 3D layer: TrackMania needs loops, jumps, wall-hugging bends and a chase camera; the 2D engine's views (mode 7,\n102\tiso) can't do loops. The handoff README says the 3D engine isn't in the package but is in the repository and the site;\n103\tTASK.md lets you add any system and change the engine. The 3D engine runs **on top of** the 2D engine (it's how the\n104\tengine's own 3D kits are built), so input, menus, settings, audio and tests stay the engine's.\n105\t\n106\tWhy not the engine's `world.vehicle`: it is a rolling ball steered on the ground plane (decision D62 in the repo's\n107\t`docs/DECISIONS.md`) — it cannot drive a loop. Why not Rapier's ray-cast vehicle controller: D62 measured it landing\n108\ton its roof after a hump, and a sprung rigid body spike for this plan bounced off a 12 m loop at 150 km/h. The car is a\n109\t**kinematic surface-following arcade model** that was prototyped and validated headless for this plan (§4.4): it\n110\tholds a 12 m loop at ~110 km/h entry (≈80 km/h at the top), exits upright, launches off a kicker (2.4 s airtime) and\n111\tlands cleanly.\n112\t\n113\t### 2.2 Getting the engine files\n114\t\n115\t```sh\n116\tgit clone https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git <scratch>/my-3d2dge && git -C <scratch>/my-3d2dge checkout 4390a98\n117\t# into the dev folder:\n118\tengine/my-3d2dge.js                 <- dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js          (2D engine, all modules, classic script)\n119\tengine/my-3d2dge-3d.js              <- engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js             (3D engine, ES module; header = its manual)\n120\tengine/vendor/three-0.182.0/        <- vendor/three-0.182.0/              (three.core/webgpu/tsl .min.js)\n121\tengine/vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js <- vendor/...         (ESM, wasm embedded as base64)\n122\tengine/docs/                        <- dist/handoff/{API.md,API-INDEX.md,AI_GUIDE.md,docs/ASSETS.md}, docs/ENGINE-3D.md\n123\tengine/tools/                       <- dist/handoff/tools/{check.mjs,filmstrip.mjs,asset-find.mjs,asset-sheet.mjs,asset-set.mjs,asset-lib.mjs}\n124\tengine/assets/                      <- dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/<set>.js (only the sets you load; see §7.4)\n125\thub/session.js                      <- ai-hub lib/hub/session.js (paste verbatim, never edit)\n126\ttools/hub/{harness.html,bridge.js,round.js} <- ai-hub lib/hub/ (test harness only; not shipped)\n127\t```\n128\tRead, in this order: the 3D engine's header (lines 1–260 of `my-3d2dge-3d.js`), `API.md` (whole), `docs/ENGINE-3D.md`,\n129\t`docs/ASSETS.md`, then grep `API-INDEX.md` as needed. The 3D starter slices in the repo (`src/starter3d/00-shell.js`,\n130\t`50-racing.js`) show the idioms (`W3 = E.World3D`, boot steps, `world.pen`, split screen).\n131\t\n132\t### 2.3 Units, axes and conventions (everyone must use these)\n133\t\n134\t- **Engine units:** 16 units = 1 metre. **x east, y south, z up.** Angles in radians from +x toward +y (0 = east,\n135\t  π/2 = south). All simulation state is in engine units; tuning tables are in metres, m/s, km/h and degrees and are\n136\t  converted once when loaded (`U = 16`).\n137\t- **Frames:** a car/block frame is `(f, r, u)` = forward, right, up, unit vectors in engine axes, with\n138\t  **`u = f × r`** and **`r = u × f`** (x east, y south, z up is left-handed as drawn; the cross-product formulas are the\n139\t  usual ones). Facing east, right is +y (south).\n140\t- **three.js** works in metres with y up: `E.World3D.toThree(x, y, z) = (x/16, z/16, y/16)`. That swap is a\n141\t  reflection, so **a triangle wound for Rapier (its normal `(b-a)×(c-a)` along the surface's up, engine axes) must be\n142\t  wound the other way (a, c, b) for three.js**, or three culls the road from above (the engine's own note on\n143\t  `world.track`). A test checks both (§9).\n144\t- **Track grid:** cell = **32 m × 32 m** (512 units), level = **8 m** (128 units). Grid **32 × 32 cells × 16\n145\t  levels**; cell (cx, cy) spans x ∈ [32·cx, 32·cx+32] m, y likewise; level L sits at z = 8·L m. Headings N/E/S/W;\n146\t  N = −y.\n147\t- **Time:** the simulation runs in the engine's fixed steps (1/120 s). Race times are **integer step counts**;\n148\t  display `M:SS.cc` (hundredths, rounded down); ties broken by steps. Store milliseconds as `steps * 1000 / 120`.\n149\t- **Randomness:** gameplay never calls `Math.random`; use `E.rng(seed)` streams (deco scatter, rival variation,\n150\t  bot jitter). Cosmetic FX may use the engine's particle randomness. (In hub sessions `Math.random` is seeded anyway.)\n151\t\n152\t### 2.4 Engine changes (make them first, list them in `engine/CHANGES.md`, mention them in Engine Feedback)\n153\t\n154\t| # | File | Change | Why |\n155\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n156\t| E1 | 3D, `class Pen` | add `frame(x, y, z, f, r, u, k = 1)`: sets `ox/oy/oz`, `k`, `ca=1, sa=0` and `this.M = [f[0], r[0], u[0], f[1], r[1], u[1], f[2], r[2], u[2]]` (same layout `frameOf()` returns) | draw a car in any orientation (upside down in a loop) without Euler singularities |\n157\t| E2 | 3D, `Camera3D.place()` and the `'fixed'` pose | if the pose has `up: [x, y, z]` (engine axes) use `cam.up.set(up[0], up[2], up[1])` before `lookAt`, and skip `rotateZ(roll)`; make sure the fixed pose object passes `up` through | a chase camera that follows the car round a loop |\n158\t| E3 | 3D, `makeBatches()` + `Pen` + the world's draw | a second set of tube/ball/box/cone batches with a transparent toon material (`transparent: true, opacity: .42, depthWrite: false`, no outline shell); `pen.ghost = true` routes parts into them; begin/end them with the others | translucent ghost cars (TrackMania's ghosts). Materials made with the world, never mid-game |\n159\t| E4 | 2D, `Input.tick` | keep `this.padValues = p.buttons.map(b => b.value ?? (b.pressed ? 1 : 0))`; add `analog(action)`: max over the action's codes of `padValues[n]` for `PadN`, and `1` for any held key/mouse code | analog R2/L2 throttle and brake |\n160\t| E5 | 2D, input + settings | `this.glyphs = 'playstation'` by default; the `glyphs` setting's options become `[['playstation','PlayStation'], ['xbox','Xbox']]`, default `'playstation'` | TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, Xbox as the option |\n161\t| E6 | 3D, `World3D` statics | export `World3D.TOON_BANDS = TOON_BANDS` | track/prop materials in the same toon bands as the pen's parts |\n162\t\n163\tDo **not** fork more of the engine than needed; when an engine API does what a section says, use it.\n164\t\n165\t### 2.5 Folder layout and modules\n166\t\n167\t```\n168\tCrackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810/\n169\t  package.json          { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"esbuild\": \"^0.25\", \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" } }\n170\t  engine/               (2.2) + CHANGES.md\n171\t  hub/session.js\n172\t  src/\n173\t    entry.js            browser entry: import '../engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js'; import { boot } from './main.js'; boot()\n174\t    main.js             createGame({ headless }) -> { game, api }: E.Game, input maps, settings, scenes, boot steps,\n175\t                        deep links (#race/D3 ...), session detection, game.debug\n176\t    config.js           TUNING (defaults, metres) + CFG (live, engine units) + reset(); every dev slider reads CFG\n177\t    lib/v3.js           vec3/quat/frame helpers in engine axes (cross, rotateAbout, slerp, orthonormalize, quatFromFrame)\n178\t    track/blocks.js     the block catalogue (§4.1): footprint, ports, path(s) -> frame, surfaces, gates, costs, env\n179\t    track/geometry.js   ribbon + cross-section mesher -> { render: {pos, uv, idx}[] per material, collide: {pos, idx, surface} }\n180\t    track/track.js      Track: occupancy (cx,cy,L), place/remove/rotate, port links, jump links, route extraction,\n181\t                        validation state, serialize (JSON) / share code (base64url) / deserialize\n182\t    track/turtle.js     turtle program -> placements (content format, §6.1)\n183\t    track/physics.js    TrackPhysics: own RAPIER.World, colliders per block (handle -> {surface, blockId}), base\n184\t                        plate + chasms, boundary walls, prop colliders; ray(), shapeCast(), groundBelow()\n185\t    track/view.js       TrackView: three meshes per block (pre-made materials per env), base plate, supports,\n186\t                        rails; gates/boards/boost chevrons drawn with the pen each frame (animated)\n187\t    track/deco.js       seeded scenery scatter + trackside dressing (turn-warning chevrons, tyre stacks, stands)\n188\t    env/envs.js         DUSTBOWL / HEDGEROW / FROSTPEAK: terrain height+colour, sky, fog, sun, ambient, palettes,\n189\t                        textures, cross-section style, support style, props, car, music\n190\t    env/textures.js     pixel-art texture bakers (asphalt, lines, curbs, dirt, ice, snow, sand, grass, rock strata)\n191\t    car/sim.js          CarSim (§4.4): the validated model + steering, grip, drift, surfaces, boost, walls, landing,\n192\t                        flop, lane assist; deterministic; no rendering\n193\t    car/draw.js         pen models of Coyote/Pebble/Moose, paint patterns, wheels, suspension, lights, effects\n194\t    car/camera.js       chase/close/hood/TV/orbit/free cameras (loop-safe up vector, FOV by speed)\n195\t    car/sound.js        engine/tyre/wind/scrape synth (WebAudio via game.audio.ctx; no-op headless)\n196\t    race/run.js         Run: countdown, step timer, gates (CP any order, finish), respawn/restart, splits, events\n197\t    race/ghost.js       record 30 Hz, int16 delta + smallest-three quaternion, base64; playback with interpolation\n198\t    race/bot.js         autopilot: route sampling, racing line, speed profile, pure-pursuit control, skill, cheats\n199\t    race/medals.js      thresholds from author time; baked table import\n200\t    modes/campaign.js   env/track select, medals, rewards, unlocks, story beats\n201\t    modes/puzzle.js     puzzle defs: fixed blocks, inventory, build zone, no-build cells; reward rules\n202\t    modes/survival.js   gauntlets, rival generation, ranks\n203\t    modes/hotseat.js    players, turns, attempts, cup standings\n204\t    modes/split.js      2–4 local players, viewports, join flow\n205\t    modes/online.js     AI Hub session flow (ghost mode), own card, ghost traffic, reporting\n206\t    modes/replay.js     replay viewer\n207\t    modes/garage.js     paint shop\n208\t    editor/editor.js    editor scene (also hosts puzzles): cursor, palette, costs, undo/redo, test, validate, slots, codes\n209\t    editor/palette.js   palette categories, block icons (baked top-down thumbnails)\n210\t    ui/kit.js           10-foot UI kit: focus grid (spatial nav, hover focus, click), tiles, buttons, sliders,\n211\t                        letter wheel, toasts, panels, prompt bar\n212\t    ui/glyphs.js        PlayStation / Xbox / keyboard / mouse glyph sprites; prompt(action) by last device\n213\t    ui/hud.js           race HUD (single and per viewport)\n214\t    ui/screens.js       title, main menu, env/track select, results, multiplayer, replays list, about, touch gate\n215\t    ui/dev.js           Dev Mode overlay (tools, tuning sliders, info)\n216\t    story/story.js      characters, beats, first-time tips, dialog runner (E.Dialog + babble)\n217\t    audio/music.js      songs (engine notation) and stings\n218\t    audio/babble.js     gibberish voice synth\n219\t    attract/attract.js  idle detection + attract sequence\n220\t    data/tracks.js      15 race tracks (turtle programs + metadata)\n221\t    data/puzzles.js     6 puzzles\n222\t    data/medals.json    baked author times (tools/bake.mjs writes it)\n223\t    data/text.js        every string the player reads (tone guide §8.3)\n224\t    save/profile.js     profile in E.store (versioned, migrations), records, ghosts, slots, settings glue\n225\t  tools/\n226\t    build.mjs           esbuild + inline -> ../Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810.html\n227\t    test.mjs            THE one command (§9): node tools/test.mjs [suite ...] [--browser] [--hub]\n228\t    node-env.mjs        headless loader (§9.1: import hooks + document stub + engine load)\n229\t    tests/*.mjs         suites\n230\t    bake.mjs            runs the bot on every track/puzzle -> src/data/medals.json\n231\t    browser.mjs         Playwright checks of the built HTML (file://)\n232\t    hub.mjs             serves 127.0.0.1:0 and runs the AI Hub harness\n233\t    hub/                harness.html, bridge.js, round.js\n234\t```\n235\t\n236\tAll game modules are ES modules that read the engine from `globalThis.My3D2dge` (`const E = globalThis.My3D2dge`).\n237\t`main.js` must not touch `document`, `window` or `location` when `headless` (guard with `typeof`), so tests can\n238\timport it.\n239\t\n240\t### 2.6 Runtime structure\n241\t\n242\t```\n243\tboot (E.Game boot steps, engine boot screen with our messages)\n244\t  1 'Starting the engines'      await E.World3D.init(game)\n245\t  2 'Reading your profile'      profile.load()\n246\t  3 'Pouring asphalt'           envs: for each of 3 environments -> new E.World3D(game, { terrain, sky, fog, sun,\n247\t                                ambient, camera: { mode: 'fixed', far }, onDraw }); materials + textures; TrackView;\n248\t                                TrackPhysics (own RAPIER.World); pen batches incl. ghost (E3)\n249\t  4 'Building the garage'       garage world (a small level-rows workshop, §5.9)\n250\t  5 'Compiling tracks'          turtle programs -> Track objects; puzzles; checks in dev builds\n251\t  6 'Teaching cacti to wave'    title showcase: load D3 into Dustbowl, start the demo bot (cheat mode)\n252\t  7 'Building the shaders'      world.prepare() for every world\n253\tscenes (E.Game scenes; pausable where noted)\n254\t  title, menu (front-end pages: main, env select, track select, puzzle select, survival select, multiplayer,\n255\t               hot seat setup, split join, replays list, garage entry, options shortcut, about),\n256\t  attract, race (pausable: single race, puzzle validation run, survival round, hot seat turn, split race, online\n257\t               round, editor test drive), results, editor (pausable; also puzzles), replay (pausable), garage,\n258\t  story (dialog over the current backdrop), credits, touchgate\n259\t```\n260\tOne `World3D` per environment lives for the whole session (switching environments = switching which world draws and"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800","uuid":"ad436043-1a57-449c-92f4-62de491e7b7f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:39:11.559Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174810__xbd1c73/work/PLAN.md","content":"# CRACKMANIA — build plan\n\n**Title: CRACKMANIA** (\"crack the clock\"). Picked as TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"TrackMania\" were brainstormed, then\none was drawn with an OS-entropy RNG (`python3 -c \"import secrets; secrets.randbelow(50)\"` → index 8 → *Crackmania*).\nThe name works three ways and the game leans on all of them: a *crack* driver (an expert), *cracking* a track's\nclock, and the puzzle mode's *cracks* (gaps in a track you fill with blocks). The finale track is called \"The Big\nCrack\" (a jump over a canyon).\n\nThis plan is for the developers (possibly other AI models) who will build the game from TASK.md and this file alone.\nRead sections 0–3 whole before writing code; sections 4–9 are the reference you build each system from.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Deliverables, names and ground rules\n\n| What | Exact name / place |\n| --- | --- |\n| Game title (title screen, About, hub tags) | `CRACKMANIA` (display), `Crackmania` (file-safe) |\n| SOURCE token in file names | `TrackMania2003` |\n| Dev folder (all source, tools, tests) | `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810/` in the folder that holds TASK.md and PLAN.md |\n| The single-file game | `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810.html` in that same root folder (beside TASK.md) |\n| Exit interview | `exit-interview.json` in the root, beside the HTML (shape in TASK.md; must parse as JSON) |\n| Engine source | `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge`, commit `4390a98` (v0.85.2): `dist/handoff/` (2D engine, docs, tools) **plus** `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` and `vendor/` (the 3D layer; see §2.2) |\n| AI Hub client | `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub`, `lib/hub/` at commit `f8835e4` (protocol 1, client 1.4.0) |\n\nGround rules from TASK.md that shape every decision:\n- Everything is drawn and synthesized by code (no image or sound files). Voices are Animalese-style gibberish.\n- No TODOs, placeholders or stubs in the shipped game. Every feature listed here ships working.\n- Other agents share this machine: work only inside the dev folder (plus a scratch dir of your own), never kill\n  processes you did not start, bind servers to `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind\n  127.0.0.1`), and stop them by PID. During planning `/tmp` (a 9.8 GB tmpfs) was **full**; put clones, npm caches and\n  build output on the main disk (e.g. `npm_config_cache=<devfolder>/.npm-cache`, `TMPDIR=<devfolder>/.tmp`).\n- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§9). Screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n- Final chat reply and the in-game About/Credits screen carry Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and\n  Prompt Feedback (TASK.md lists the fields; \"Plan\" = `PLAN.md`). Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game in one page\n\n**Pitch.** An original arcade time-attack stunt racer in the spirit of TrackMania (Nadeo, PC, 21 Nov 2003). Short\ntracks built from blocks (loops, kickers, boost pads, banked bends), a clock you race against, a ghost of your best\nrun, medals, coppers to spend on track pieces, and an editor where every track — yours or ours — is just blocks on a\ngrid. Three environments with their own car and handling. Original names, art, music, tracks and characters.\n\n**What the source is (research done for this plan).** TrackMania (2003): developer Nadeo; publishers Focus Home\nInteractive (FR), Digital Jesters (UK), Enlight (US, June 2004); director Florent Castelnérac; music Henri-Pierre\nPellegrin. Solo modes **Race, Puzzle and Survival** (Survival — three computer ghost cars, finish last and you're out,\nfirst or second ranks you up — arrived with the Power Up! update), each with tracks in every environment; three\nenvironments **Desert, Rally (countryside) and Snow (alpine)**, each with its own car (muscle \"speed\" cars, rally\nsuper-minis, alpine 4x4s); a **track editor** whose grid-snapped blocks (\"bricks\": roads, checkpoints, long bends,\nloop-the-loops, jumps) are **bought with coppers** earned from bronze/silver/gold medals; a **car painter**;\nmultiplayer by **hot seat, LAN and Internet**; tracks and skins shared online. Metacritic 74. TrackMania's DNA, which\nthis game must capture: *the clock is the enemy*, *instant respawn/restart*, *ghosts and checkpoint splits*, *stunts\nare the route*, *tracks are toys anyone builds*, *terse, playful presentation*.\n\n**Genre:** arcade racing (time attack) + track-building puzzle. **Perspective:** third-person chase camera (far\nchase, close chase, hood cam) in races; an orbiting three-quarter overhead camera over a 3D grid in the editor and\npuzzles; trackside TV cameras in replays.\n\n**Look:** the engine's native style — low-resolution 3D (the game's 320x240-class buffer, `expand: true`), toon\nshading with ink outlines on code-drawn parts, crisp nearest-filtered pixel textures baked by code, PS1/N64-era\nracer. Bright TrackMania colours: red/white curbs, chunky gates with flip-digit time boards, chevron boost pads.\n\n**Modes (all ship):**\n1. **Race** — 15 tracks (5 per environment), medals Bronze/Silver/Gold/Author, coppers, unlocks.\n2. **Puzzle** — 6 puzzles: start, checkpoints and finish are placed; you get a limited block inventory; build a route,\n   then drive it. Faster routes earn better medals.\n3. **Survival** — per environment, a 5-round gauntlet against three ghost rivals (the \"Ghost Guild\"); last place is\n   out, 1st/2nd rank you up.\n4. **Build** — the track editor (place/rotate/raise/remove blocks, coppers cost, undo/redo, test drive, validate by\n   driving, 6 save slots, share codes).\n5. **Hot Seat** — 2–8 players take turns on one controller/keyboard; standings over a cup of tracks.\n6. **Split Screen** — 2–4 local players at once (pads or keyboard halves); cars are ghosts to each other.\n7. **Online** — AI Hub sessions in the hub's `ghost` mode: everyone time-attacks the same track for 3 minutes and sees\n   the others as ghost cars (TrackMania's own online model).\n8. **Replays** — watch any run with chase/TV/orbit cameras, scrub, save to 10 slots.\n9. **Garage** — paint shop for the three cars (colours, 8 patterns, number, rims).\nPlus: title + attract mode, Dev Mode (pause menu), Settings, About/Credits, touch-only gate.\n\n**Characters (original):** **Coco Asphalte**, a cheerful track architect in a hard hat (host, tutorials, story);\n**Monsieur Drapeau**, the flag man at every start gate; the **Ghost Guild** rivals — **Tex Mirage** (cocky),\n**Mlle Gravillon** (precise), **Bobo Yeti** (chaotic). Cars: **Coyote** (Dustbowl muscle car), **Pebble** (Hedgerow\nrally hatch), **Moose** (Frostpeak 4x4). Environments: **Dustbowl** (desert: hot asphalt, big air), **Hedgerow**\n(countryside: tight lanes, loose gravel), **Frostpeak** (alpine: thin air, thin ice).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Technology and architecture\n\n### 2.1 Stack (decided)\n\n| Layer | Choice |\n| --- | --- |\n| Game shell | my-3D2dge **2D engine** v0.85.2 (`dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js`, classic script, every module): fixed 1/120 s steps, scenes, input with rebinding/glyphs, pause menu, settings, boot screen, audio synth, particles, rigs (`E.Humanoid`), pixel font/UI, `E.store`, `game.advance`/`game.debug` for headless tests |\n| 3D | my-3D2dge **3D engine** (`engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` from the repo, ES module, same commit): `E.World3D` (terrain backdrop, sky, fog, sun + shadows, the **pen** for code-drawn parts, particles in 3D, cameras incl. split screen, filters, `world.shape` props, rig actors) on **three.js r182** (WebGPURenderer, falls back to WebGL 2) |\n| Physics queries | **Rapier 0.19.3** (`@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat`, WASM embedded) — our **own** `RAPIER.World` holding the track's fixed colliders; used only for ray casts and shape casts. The car is **not** a Rapier body (§4.4) |\n| Bundling | **esbuild** (dev dependency) bundles three + Rapier + the 3D engine + game modules into one inline `<script type=\"module\">`; the 2D engine is inlined as a classic `<script>` before it; AI Hub `session.js` is the first script in `<head>` |\n| Tools | Node 24 (present), Playwright 1.63.0 (as the handoff pins) + Chromium for browser checks, Python 3 for the hub harness server |\n\nWhy the 3D layer: TrackMania needs loops, jumps, wall-hugging bends and a chase camera; the 2D engine's views (mode 7,\niso) can't do loops. The handoff README says the 3D engine isn't in the package but is in the repository and the site;\nTASK.md lets you add any system and change the engine. The 3D engine runs **on top of** the 2D engine (it's how the\nengine's own 3D kits are built), so input, menus, settings, audio and tests stay the engine's.\n\nWhy not the engine's `world.vehicle`: it is a rolling ball steered on the ground plane (decision D62 in the repo's\n`docs/DECISIONS.md`) — it cannot drive a loop. Why not Rapier's ray-cast vehicle controller: D62 measured it landing\non its roof after a hump, and a sprung rigid body spike for this plan bounced off a 12 m loop at 150 km/h. The car is a\n**kinematic surface-following arcade model** that was prototyped and validated headless for this plan (§4.4): it\nholds a 12 m loop at ~110 km/h entry (≈80 km/h at the top), exits upright, launches off a kicker (2.4 s airtime) and\nlands cleanly.\n\n### 2.2 Getting the engine files\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git <scratch>/my-3d2dge && git -C <scratch>/my-3d2dge checkout 4390a98\n# into the dev folder:\nengine/my-3d2dge.js                 <- dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js          (2D engine, all modules, classic script)\nengine/my-3d2dge-3d.js              <- engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js             (3D engine, ES module; header = its manual)\nengine/vendor/three-0.182.0/        <- vendor/three-0.182.0/              (three.core/webgpu/tsl .min.js)\nengine/vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js <- vendor/...         (ESM, wasm embedded as base64)\nengine/docs/                        <- dist/handoff/{API.md,API-INDEX.md,AI_GUIDE.md,docs/ASSETS.md}, docs/ENGINE-3D.md\nengine/tools/                       <- dist/handoff/tools/{check.mjs,filmstrip.mjs,asset-find.mjs,asset-sheet.mjs,asset-set.mjs,asset-lib.mjs}\nengine/assets/                      <- dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/<set>.js (only the sets you load; see §7.4)\nhub/session.js                      <- ai-hub lib/hub/session.js (paste verbatim, never edit)\ntools/hub/{harness.html,bridge.js,round.js} <- ai-hub lib/hub/ (test harness only; not shipped)\n```\nRead, in this order: the 3D engine's header (lines 1–260 of `my-3d2dge-3d.js`), `API.md` (whole), `docs/ENGINE-3D.md`,\n`docs/ASSETS.md`, then grep `API-INDEX.md` as needed. The 3D starter slices in the repo (`src/starter3d/00-shell.js`,\n`50-racing.js`) show the idioms (`W3 = E.World3D`, boot steps, `world.pen`, split screen).\n\n### 2.3 Units, axes and conventions (everyone must use these)\n\n- **Engine units:** 16 units = 1 metre. **x east, y south, z up.** Angles in radians from +x toward +y (0 = east,\n  π/2 = south). All simulation state is in engine units; tuning tables are in metres, m/s, km/h and degrees and are\n  converted once when loaded (`U = 16`).\n- **Frames:** a car/block frame is `(f, r, u)` = forward, right, up, unit vectors in engine axes, with\n  **`u = f × r`** and **`r = u × f`** (x east, y south, z up is left-handed as drawn; the cross-product formulas are the\n  usual ones). Facing east, right is +y (south).\n- **three.js** works in metres with y up: `E.World3D.toThree(x, y, z) = (x/16, z/16, y/16)`. That swap is a\n  reflection, so **a triangle wound for Rapier (its normal `(b-a)×(c-a)` along the surface's up, engine axes) must be\n  wound the other way (a, c, b) for three.js**, or three culls the road from above (the engine's own note on\n  `world.track`). A test checks both (§9).\n- **Track grid:** cell = **32 m × 32 m** (512 units), level = **8 m** (128 units). Grid **32 × 32 cells × 16\n  levels**; cell (cx, cy) spans x ∈ [32·cx, 32·cx+32] m, y likewise; level L sits at z = 8·L m. Headings N/E/S/W;\n  N = −y.\n- **Time:** the simulation runs in the engine's fixed steps (1/120 s). Race times are **integer step counts**;\n  display `M:SS.cc` (hundredths, rounded down); ties broken by steps. Store milliseconds as `steps * 1000 / 120`.\n- **Randomness:** gameplay never calls `Math.random`; use `E.rng(seed)` streams (deco scatter, rival variation,\n  bot jitter). Cosmetic FX may use the engine's particle randomness. (In hub sessions `Math.random` is seeded anyway.)\n\n### 2.4 Engine changes (make them first, list them in `engine/CHANGES.md`, mention them in Engine Feedback)\n\n| # | File | Change | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| E1 | 3D, `class Pen` | add `frame(x, y, z, f, r, u, k = 1)`: sets `ox/oy/oz`, `k`, `ca=1, sa=0` and `this.M = [f[0], r[0], u[0], f[1], r[1], u[1], f[2], r[2], u[2]]` (same layout `frameOf()` returns) | draw a car in any orientation (upside down in a loop) without Euler singularities |\n| E2 | 3D, `Camera3D.place()` and the `'fixed'` pose | if the pose has `up: [x, y, z]` (engine axes) use `cam.up.set(up[0], up[2], up[1])` before `lookAt`, and skip `rotateZ(roll)`; make sure the fixed pose object passes `up` through | a chase camera that follows the car round a loop |\n| E3 | 3D, `makeBatches()` + `Pen` + the world's draw | a second set of tube/ball/box/cone batches with a transparent toon material (`transparent: true, opacity: .42, depthWrite: false`, no outline shell); `pen.ghost = true` routes parts into them; begin/end them with the others | translucent ghost cars (TrackMania's ghosts). Materials made with the world, never mid-game |\n| E4 | 2D, `Input.tick` | keep `this.padValues = p.buttons.map(b => b.value ?? (b.pressed ? 1 : 0))`; add `analog(action)`: max over the action's codes of `padValues[n]` for `PadN`, and `1` for any held key/mouse code | analog R2/L2 throttle and brake |\n| E5 | 2D, input + settings | `this.glyphs = 'playstation'` by default; the `glyphs` setting's options become `[['playstation','PlayStation'], ['xbox','Xbox']]`, default `'playstation'` | TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, Xbox as the option |\n| E6 | 3D, `World3D` statics | export `World3D.TOON_BANDS = TOON_BANDS` | track/prop materials in the same toon bands as the pen's parts |\n\nDo **not** fork more of the engine than needed; when an engine API does what a section says, use it.\n\n### 2.5 Folder layout and modules\n\n```\nCrackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810/\n  package.json          { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"esbuild\": \"^0.25\", \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" } }\n  engine/               (2.2) + CHANGES.md\n  hub/session.js\n  src/\n    entry.js            browser entry: import '../engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js'; import { boot } from './main.js'; boot()\n    main.js             createGame({ headless }) -> { game, api }: E.Game, input maps, settings, scenes, boot steps,\n                        deep links (#race/D3 ...), session detection, game.debug\n    config.js           TUNING (defaults, metres) + CFG (live, engine units) + reset(); every dev slider reads CFG\n    lib/v3.js           vec3/quat/frame helpers in engine axes (cross, rotateAbout, slerp, orthonormalize, quatFromFrame)\n    track/blocks.js     the block catalogue (§4.1): footprint, ports, path(s) -> frame, surfaces, gates, costs, env\n    track/geometry.js   ribbon + cross-section mesher -> { render: {pos, uv, idx}[] per material, collide: {pos, idx, surface} }\n    track/track.js      Track: occupancy (cx,cy,L), place/remove/rotate, port links, jump links, route extraction,\n                        validation state, serialize (JSON) / share code (base64url) / deserialize\n    track/turtle.js     turtle program -> placements (content format, §6.1)\n    track/physics.js    TrackPhysics: own RAPIER.World, colliders per block (handle -> {surface, blockId}), base\n                        plate + chasms, boundary walls, prop colliders; ray(), shapeCast(), groundBelow()\n    track/view.js       TrackView: three meshes per block (pre-made materials per env), base plate, supports,\n                        rails; gates/boards/boost chevrons drawn with the pen each frame (animated)\n    track/deco.js       seeded scenery scatter + trackside dressing (turn-warning chevrons, tyre stacks, stands)\n    env/envs.js         DUSTBOWL / HEDGEROW / FROSTPEAK: terrain height+colour, sky, fog, sun, ambient, palettes,\n                        textures, cross-section style, support style, props, car, music\n    env/textures.js     pixel-art texture bakers (asphalt, lines, curbs, dirt, ice, snow, sand, grass, rock strata)\n    car/sim.js          CarSim (§4.4): the validated model + steering, grip, drift, surfaces, boost, walls, landing,\n                        flop, lane assist; deterministic; no rendering\n    car/draw.js         pen models of Coyote/Pebble/Moose, paint patterns, wheels, suspension, lights, effects\n    car/camera.js       chase/close/hood/TV/orbit/free cameras (loop-safe up vector, FOV by speed)\n    car/sound.js        engine/tyre/wind/scrape synth (WebAudio via game.audio.ctx; no-op headless)\n    race/run.js         Run: countdown, step timer, gates (CP any order, finish), respawn/restart, splits, events\n    race/ghost.js       record 30 Hz, int16 delta + smallest-three quaternion, base64; playback with interpolation\n    race/bot.js         autopilot: route sampling, racing line, speed profile, pure-pursuit control, skill, cheats\n    race/medals.js      thresholds from author time; baked table import\n    modes/campaign.js   env/track select, medals, rewards, unlocks, story beats\n    modes/puzzle.js     puzzle defs: fixed blocks, inventory, build zone, no-build cells; reward rules\n    modes/survival.js   gauntlets, rival generation, ranks\n    modes/hotseat.js    players, turns, attempts, cup standings\n    modes/split.js      2–4 local players, viewports, join flow\n    modes/online.js     AI Hub session flow (ghost mode), own card, ghost traffic, reporting\n    modes/replay.js     replay viewer\n    modes/garage.js     paint shop\n    editor/editor.js    editor scene (also hosts puzzles): cursor, palette, costs, undo/redo, test, validate, slots, codes\n    editor/palette.js   palette categories, block icons (baked top-down thumbnails)\n    ui/kit.js           10-foot UI kit: focus grid (spatial nav, hover focus, click), tiles, buttons, sliders,\n                        letter wheel, toasts, panels, prompt bar\n    ui/glyphs.js        PlayStation / Xbox / keyboard / mouse glyph sprites; prompt(action) by last device\n    ui/hud.js           race HUD (single and per viewport)\n    ui/screens.js       title, main menu, env/track select, results, multiplayer, replays list, about, touch gate\n    ui/dev.js           Dev Mode overlay (tools, tuning sliders, info)\n    story/story.js      characters, beats, first-time tips, dialog runner (E.Dialog + babble)\n    audio/music.js      songs (engine notation) and stings\n    audio/babble.js     gibberish voice synth\n    attract/attract.js  idle detection + attract sequence\n    data/tracks.js      15 race tracks (turtle programs + metadata)\n    data/puzzles.js     6 puzzles\n    data/medals.json    baked author times (tools/bake.mjs writes it)\n    data/text.js        every string the player reads (tone guide §8.3)\n    save/profile.js     profile in E.store (versioned, migrations), records, ghosts, slots, settings glue\n  tools/\n    build.mjs           esbuild + inline -> ../Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174810.html\n    test.mjs            THE one command (§9): node tools/test.mjs [suite ...] [--browser] [--hub]\n    node-env.mjs        headless loader (§9.1: import hooks + document stub + engine load)\n    tests/*.mjs         suites\n    bake.mjs            runs the bot on every track/puzzle -> src/data/medals.json\n    browser.mjs         Playwright checks of the built HTML (file://)\n    hub.mjs             serves 127.0.0.1:0 and runs the AI Hub harness\n    hub/                harness.html, bridge.js, round.js\n```\n\nAll game modules are ES modules that read the engine from `globalThis.My3D2dge` (`const E = globalThis.My3D2dge`).\n`main.js` must not touch `document`, `window` or `location` when `headless` (guard with `typeof`), so tests can\nimport it.\n\n### 2.6 Runtime structure\n\n```\nboot (E.Game boot steps, engine boot screen with our messages)\n  1 'Starting the engines'      await E.World3D.init(game)\n  2 'Reading your profile'      profile.load()\n  3 'Pouring asphalt'           envs: for each of 3 environments -> new E.World3D(game, { terrain, sky, fog, sun,\n                                ambient, camera: { mode: 'fixed', far }, onDraw }); materials + textures; TrackView;\n                                TrackPhysics (own RAPIER.World); pen batches incl. ghost (E3)\n  4 'Building the garage'       garage world (a small level-rows workshop, §5.9)\n  5 'Compiling tracks'          turtle programs -> Track objects; puzzles; checks in dev builds\n  6 'Teaching cacti to wave'    title showcase: load D3 into Dustbowl, start the demo bot (cheat mode)\n  7 'Building the shaders'      world.prepare() for every world\nscenes (E.Game scenes; pausable where noted)\n  title, menu (front-end pages: main, env select, track select, puzzle select, survival select, multiplayer,\n               hot seat setup, split join, replays list, garage entry, options shortcut, about),\n  attract, race (pausable: single race, puzzle validation run, survival round, hot seat turn, split race, online\n               round, editor test drive), results, editor (pausable; also puzzles), replay (pausable), garage,\n  story (dialog over the current backdrop), credits, touchgate\n```\nOne `World3D` per environment lives for the whole session (switching environments = switching which world draws and","numLines":260,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1416}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WQkzQGpCAJ3vnhomwa1mxm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
