{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019UFp3Y2EYnjMghe8GG8XeM","type":"tool_result","content":"260\tOne `World3D` per environment lives for the whole session (switching environments = switching which world draws and\n261\tsteps; worlds that aren't shown don't step). Loading a track rebuilds only the track's meshes and colliders (fast;\n262\tmaterials are pre-made — never create a material after boot, it stalls a frame to build its shader).\n263\t\n264\tThe **track ground**: the World3D `terrain` provides the scenery outside the grid (dunes and mesas, farmland hills,\n265\tsnowy mountains) and sits at **−60 m under the grid**; our own **base plate** mesh covers the grid at z = 0 with holes\n266\tfor \"chasm\" cells, so a chasm shows a deep canyon whose walls are the terrain's rock strata (\"the crack\"). Set\n267\t`game.particles.ground` to our ground query after each world switch so dust and sparks land on the plate, not 60 m\n268\tbelow (check whether World3D re-assigns it; if it does, override after it).\n269\t\n270\t### 2.7 Build to a single file\n271\t\n272\t`tools/build.mjs`:\n273\t1. esbuild `entryPoints: ['src/entry.js'], bundle: true, format: 'esm', minify: true, legalComments: 'inline'`, with\n274\t   a tiny plugin that resolves `three`, `three/webgpu` → `engine/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`,\n275\t   `three/tsl` → `.../three.tsl.min.js`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat` → `engine/vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js`\n276\t   (use a plugin `onResolve`; esbuild's `alias` option does not reliably take sub-paths like `three/webgpu`).\n277\t2. Write the HTML: `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then **`<script>` + hub/session.js\n278\t   verbatim**, then the viewport meta, `<title>CRACKMANIA</title>`, the hub metas (§5.7), a `<style>` (stage + two\n279\t   canvases as the engine's 3D pages use: `#view` under `#screen`, `image-rendering: pixelated`, black background),\n280\t   `</head><body>`, the engine boot splash markup (`E.bootSplash('CRACKMANIA')` — get the string at build time by\n281\t   loading the 2D engine in Node), `<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>`,\n282\t   `<script>` + the 2D engine `</script>`, `<script type=\"module\">` + the bundle `</script>`.\n283\t3. Escape `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`. Expect ~5 MB. No network fetches at run time.\n284\t4. The file must run from `file://` (inline module scripts and base64 WASM need no server) and from the hub's server.\n285\t**Validated while planning:** exactly this pipeline (esbuild 0.25.12 with the resolve plugin, the 2D engine inline as\n286\ta classic script, one inline module with three + Rapier + the 3D engine + a test scene) produced a **4.47 MB** file\n287\tthat booted from `file://` in Playwright 1.63's headless Chromium with **zero errors**: three fell back to **WebGL 2**\n288\t(no WebGPU adapter headless), the terrain, sky, a pen-drawn outlined car with its shadow and the pixel HUD all drew.\n289\tExpected console warnings: Rapier's \"deprecated parameters for the initialization function\", \"No available\n290\tadapters\", \"WebGPURenderer: WebGPU is not available\" — not errors. **Fallback** if inlining ever breaks: keep the\n291\tbundle for the 3D engine + game only and add an import map to jsDelivr\n292\t(`https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js`, `.../three.tsl.min.js`,\n293\t`https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs`) — TASK.md allows CDN deps.\n294\t\n295\t---\n296\t\n297\t## 3. Player experience and controls\n298\t\n299\t### 3.1 The core loop (must feel instant)\n300\t\n301\tTrack select → **flyover** (2.5 s camera sweep along the route with the track name; any button skips) → **3-2-1-GO**\n302\t(start-gate lights, Monsieur Drapeau drops the flag, beeps, gibberish count) → drive → each checkpoint shows the split\n303\tand the delta to your best (blue `-0.42` ahead, red `+0.31` behind) → **FINISH** (slow-mo 0.6 s, camera swings\n304\tround) → results (time, delta, medal drop-in, coppers count-up) → **Retry is one button and takes under half a\n305\tsecond.** In play: **Respawn** puts you at the last checkpoint, standing, the clock still running (TrackMania 2003's\n306\tclassic respawn); **Restart** goes back to the start with a short 1-second \"READY... GO\". Both work at any moment,\n307\tincluding mid-air. The ghost of your best run (translucent) races with you; Coco's ghost (the author time, generated\n308\tby the bot) can be switched on in Settings.\n309\t\n310\t### 3.2 Input maps (`game.input` presets; prompts always from `game.input.label(action)` + our glyph sprites)\n311\t\n312\tRace (`RACE_INPUT`; analog via E4 `input.analog`):\n313\t\n314\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Notes |\n315\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n316\t| steer | left stick X (analog), d-pad ←/→ | ←/→, A/D | `input.move()[0]`; keyboard steering ramps (see §4.4 `steerRamp`) |\n317\t| gas | R2 (Pad7, analog), ✕ (Pad0) | ↑, W | analog value 0..1 |\n318\t| brake / reverse | L2 (Pad6, analog), □ (Pad2) | ↓, S | brake + steer at speed = drift (§4.4) |\n319\t| respawn (last CP) | ○ (Pad1) | Enter, Backspace | TrackMania habit |\n320\t| restart (from start) | △ (Pad3) | Delete, R | |\n321\t| camera (far → close → hood) | R1 (Pad5) | C (also 1/2/3 pick one) | |\n322\t| ghost on/off | L1 (Pad4) | G | |\n323\t| horn (just for fun: two-tone toot + gibberish \"beep beep\") | R3 (Pad11) | H | |\n324\t| pause | Options (Pad9) | Esc, P | engine standard |\n325\tMouse in races: none (racing doesn't aim); the cursor hides while driving and reappears on menus.\n326\t\n327\tMenus (engine `up/down/left/right/confirm/cancel` + ours): d-pad/left stick move focus; ✕/Enter/Space choose;\n328\t○/Esc/Backspace back; L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs; △/Y \"secondary\" (e.g. ghost toggle on track select, reset on a\n329\tslider); **mouse hover moves the focus, click chooses, wheel scrolls lists**. Repeat on hold (`input.repeat`).\n330\t\n331\tEditor (`EDIT_INPUT`, §5.4) and replay (`REPLAY_INPUT`, §5.8) have their own tables in their sections.\n332\t\n333\tRumble (`input.rumble`): wall hits (∝ impact), hard landings, boost pads (short), checkpoint (tiny), finish.\n334\t\n335\t**AI Hub reserved:** in a session, ignore Options (pause) while Create (Pad8) is held.\n336\t\n337\t### 3.3 10-foot UI rules (apply everywhere)\n338\t\n339\t- Buffer is `res: 'ps1', expand: true` (~427×240 on 16:9). Primary text: pixel font at **scale 2** (14 px of 240 ≈\n340\t  6% of screen height). Secondary text scale 1, never the `tiny` font for anything the player must read. Big numbers\n341\t  (timer, speed) use a custom chunky digit sprite at 2–3× (§7.6).\n342\t- Tiles at least 96×64 buffer px, 8 px gaps, 12 px safe margins (and keep clear of the engine's top-right corner\n343\t  buttons: `game.cornerArea()`).\n344\t- One focused element always visible (accent border + 1.08× scale tween + a soft glow); spatial navigation; hover =\n345\t  focus; click = choose. Every screen has a back path (○/Esc/right-click).\n346\t- A **prompt bar** along the bottom of every screen: `[glyph] Select  [glyph] Back  [glyph] ...` — glyphs follow\n347\t  `input.lastDevice` (PlayStation shapes by default; Xbox letters if the Settings \"Button symbols\" is Xbox; keycaps\n348\t  for keyboard; mouse icons when the mouse was last used).\n349\t- No dense data screens: at most 6 numbers on any results/records panel; lists show 5–7 rows at scale 2 with\n350\t  scrolling.\n351\t- Colour is never the only signal (deltas carry `+`/`−`, medals carry letters B/S/G/A on their icons).\n352\t\n353\t---\n354\t\n355\t## 4. Core systems\n356\t\n357\t### 4.1 Blocks (the catalogue)\n358\t\n359\tEvery track — campaign, puzzle, editor — is a list of placements `{ type, cx, cy, L, rot }` (rot = heading of the\n360\tblock's local +x: 0 E, 1 S, 2 W, 3 N). In its **local frame** a block's entry is the centre of the west face of local\n361\tcell (0,0) at level 0, heading +x; local y is to the right (south when rot = 0). Footprint offsets below are\n362\t`(along, right, levels)`.\n363\t\n364\t| Type | Token | Footprint (along × right × levels) | Exit (local cell, heading) | Geometry | Surface | Cost (¢) | Envs |\n365\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n366\t| Start | `ST` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) → +x | straight; spawn at s = 8 m; start arch at s = 10 m | road | 0 (max 1) | all |\n367\t| Finish | `FN` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) → +x | straight; finish arch at s = 16 m | road | 0 (≥1) | all |\n368\t| Checkpoint | `CP` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) | straight; gate at s = 16 m | road | 15 | all |\n369\t| Straight | `S` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) | straight | road | 5 | all |\n370\t| Boost | `BO` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) | straight + boost pad (s 10–22 m) | road+boost | 40 | all |\n371\t| Turn, tight | `L1`/`R1` | 1×1×1 | side cell | quarter circle r = 16 m | road | 8 | all |\n372\t| Turn, wide | `L2`/`R2` | 2×2×1 | (1, ±2) heading ±y | quarter circle r = 48 m | road | 15 | all |\n373\t| Turn, sweeping | `L3`/`R3` | 3×3×1 | (2, ±3) heading ±y | quarter circle r = 80 m | road | 25 | all |\n374\t| Banked turn | `BL2`/`BR2` | 2×2×2 | as L2/R2 | r = 48 m, bank 0→28°→0 (sin ramp) | road | 30 | Dustbowl, Frostpeak |\n375\t| Hill up/down (steep) | `U1`/`D1` | 1×1×2 | (1,0), level ±1 | smoothstep rise 8 m over 32 m (max grade 37.5%: launches you at speed) | road | 10 | all |\n376\t| Hill up/down | `U2`/`D2` | 2×1×2 | (2,0), level ±1 | smoothstep rise 8 m over 64 m (max 18.75%) | road | 15 | all |\n377\t| Kicker | `KI` | 1×1×2 | none (air) | concave arc to a **15° lip, 4.2 m high** at s = 32 m (arc radius 123.6 m) | road | 30 | all |\n378\t| Landing | `LA` | 2×1×2 | (2,0) | air entry; smoothstep descent from +3 m to 0 over 64 m | road | 20 | all |\n379\t| Gap | `GAPn` | n×1×2 reserved air | — | nothing (a reserved corridor); at level 0 the base plate is cut: **chasm** | — | 0 | all |\n380\t| Loop | `LOOPR`/`LOOPL` | 2×2×4 | (2, ±1) → +x | §4.2: 12 m-radius loop with a one-cell sideways shift; lane assist | road | 120 | all |\n381\t| Crossing | `CR` | 1×1×1 | all four faces | flat square deck, 4 ports | road | 20 | all |\n382\t| Bumps | `BU` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) | three 0.5 m humps (`z = 0.5·sin²(3πs/32)`) | road | 10 | Hedgerow |\n383\t| Dirt straight / turns | `DS`, `DL1`/`DR1`, `DL2`/`DR2` | as S / L1 / L2 | | | dirt | as base | Hedgerow, Dustbowl |\n384\t| Ice straight / turns | `IS`, `IL1`/`IR1`, `IL2`/`IR2` | as S / L1 / L2 | | | ice | as base | Frostpeak |\n385\t| Snow road variants | `SN` (straight) | as S | | | snow | 5 | Frostpeak |\n386\t| Deco items | (editor only) | 1×1 | — | scenery props from §7.4 | — | 5–25 | per env |\n387\t\n388\tTurtle rules (§6.1) for where the next block goes follow from the \"Exit\" column: a turn of size n fills the n×n square\n389\t`c0 + a·h + b·s` (a, b ∈ [0, n)) and exits into `c0 + (n−1)·h + n·s` heading `s` (s = the turn side); a loop fills a\n390\t2×2×4 box and exits into `c0 + 2h + s` with the heading unchanged; hills occupy their cells on both levels they span;\n391\ta kicker reserves the level above its cell; `GAPn` reserves n cells on levels L and L+1.\n392\t\n393\t**Ports and links.** A port = (world cell, face, level, heading). Two blocks connect when a port of one and a port of\n394\tthe other share the face, level, and opposite headings. Straight-like blocks are symmetric (drivable both ways); turns,\n395\thills, kickers, landings, loops, start and finish are directional for the route (a car can still drive them\n396\tbackwards physically). **Jump links:** a kicker links to the first landing (or any block whose entry faces it) found\n397\talong its heading within 5 cells, on the kicker's level or up to 3 levels lower. The **route** is the chain of links\n398\tfrom Start to Finish (§4.8); the editor shows unlinked ports as red arrows.\n399\t\n400\t### 4.2 Block paths (centrelines and frames)\n401\t\n402\tEach block type exports `path(s) -> { p, t, u }` for s ∈ [0, len] in local metres (p position, t unit tangent, u unit\n403\tup), plus `width(s)`, `bank`, and `surface(s)`. Right is `r = u × t`. Formulas (local frame, entry at (0,0,0), +x\n404\tforward, +y right):\n405\t\n406\t- **Straight** (n cells): `p = (s, 0, 0)`, `t = (1,0,0)`, `u = (0,0,1)`, `len = 32n`.\n407\t- **Turn R, radius ρ = 32n − 16** (n = 1, 2, 3): `φ = s/ρ`, `p = (ρ sin φ, ρ(1 − cos φ), 0)`, `t = (cos φ, sin φ, 0)`,\n408\t  `len = πρ/2`. L mirrors y. Check: n = 1 exits at (16, 16) — the centre of the south face of cell (0,0).\n409\t- **Banked**: as the turn; bank `β(φ) = 28°·sin(π·φ/(π/2))`; `u = cos β·ẑ + sin β·r̂_in` where `r̂_in` points to the\n410\t  turn centre (right for R). The road edge on the outside rises; the ports stay flat.\n411\t- **Hill Un** (rise H = 8 m over len = 32n): `z(s) = H·(3x² − 2x³)`, x = s/len; `t ∝ (1, 0, z'(s))`;\n412\t  `u ∝ (−z'(s), 0, 1)`. D mirrors (descends). The U1 crest has radius ≈ 21 m: above ~60 km/h the car takes off (by\n413\t  design); U2's crest (≈ 85 m) holds the car up to ~150 km/h.\n414\t- **Kicker**: `z(s) = ρk − sqrt(ρk² − s²)`, ρk = 123.6 m, s ∈ [0, 32]: lip angle 15°, lip height 4.2 m.\n415\t- **Landing**: `z(s) = 3·(1 − (3x² − 2x³))`, x = s/64.\n416\t- **Loop R** (exit one cell right, two cells ahead, same heading). Let `S = 32 + 2π·12 + 32` (≈ 139.4 m) be the\n417\t  total centreline length and `y(s) = 32·(3q² − 2q³)` with q = s/S (the sideways shift spread over the whole piece).\n418\t  For s ∈ [0, 32]: `p = (s, y, 0)`; for the circle (θ = (s − 32)/12 ∈ [0, 2π]): `p = (32 + 12 sin θ, y, 12(1 − cos θ))`,\n419\t  `u0 = (−sin θ, 0, cos θ)`; for s ∈ [32 + 24π, S]: `p = (32 + (s − 32 − 24π), y, 0)` — i.e. the circle's bottom is at\n420\t  local x = 32, entering and leaving there, shifted sideways. Tangent = normalized derivative (numerical, h = 0.01 m);\n421\t  up = `u0` Gram–Schmidt'ed against t. At the circle's bottom points the entry and exit lanes are ~23 m apart, more\n422\t  than a road width, so the ribbons never touch. L mirrors y. The loop block has **lane assist** (§4.4 step 7).\n423\t- **Crossing**: a flat 32×32 m deck; its \"path\" for routing is the straight line through the two opposite ports the\n424\t  route uses.\n425\t\n426\tPort frames must match exactly between linked blocks (position within 1 cm, tangent and up within 0.5°): a test checks\n427\tevery link of every shipped track (§9).\n428\t\n429\t### 4.3 Geometry, materials and colliders\n430\t\n431\t**Ribbon mesher.** Sample each block path every 1 m (curves) or per cell (straights; 4 segments), and at each sample\n432\tlay the environment's **cross-section** (a list of (lateral offset, height, uv.u, part) points) along `r` and `u`.\n433\tParts: `road`, `line` (baked into the road texture), `curb`, `rail` (vertical barrier), `bank` (snowbank / hedge /\n434\tstone wall), `underside`. Output per part: render triangles (three winding) + UVs (u across in metres/2, v along in\n435\tmetres/4 so the pixel textures tile at ~8–12 cm per texel) and collision triangles (Rapier winding, engine units) with a\n436\tsurface tag.\n437\t\n438\t| Env | Road width | Curb | Elevated edge | Ground-level edge | Supports under elevated pieces |\n439\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n440\t| Dustbowl | 14 m asphalt, yellow centre dashes, white edges | 1 m red/white, +0.2 m, drivable | steel guard rail 0.9 m (solid) | sand (base plate) | concrete pillars 1.4 m square every 16 m along both edges |\n441\t| Hedgerow | 12 m tarmac (or dirt), white edges | 0.8 m white/green | low stone wall 0.8 m (solid) | grass verge; hedges on some pieces (deco) | earth embankment (45° grassy slopes, drivable, slow) |\n442\t| Frostpeak | 12 m packed-snow road (or ice), orange edge poles every 8 m (deco) | 0.8 m blue/white | snowbank 1.2 m (sloped, low grip: pushes you back) | snow | rock-and-snow embankment |\n443\t\n444\t- Turns and banked turns get the elevated-edge barrier on their **outer** edge even at ground level (Dustbowl: tyre\n445\t  wall instead of rail).\n446\t- Loops get 0.5 m lips both sides. Kickers and landings get curbs only. Gaps get nothing.\n447\t- **Base plate** (per track): one mesh of the grid's cells minus chasm cells (GAP cells on level 0); environment ground\n448\t  texture (sand ripples / mown grass stripes / snow with sparkles). Collider: one trimesh, surface = the env's\n449\t  off-road surface. **Chasm walls**: for each chasm cell edge that borders plate, a vertical quad down 60 m with the\n450\t  terrain rock texture. **Boundary**: a 2 m wall all round the grid (Dustbowl: striped concrete barriers; Hedgerow: dry\n451\t  stone wall; Frostpeak: snow fence) with a collider.\n452\t- **Materials** (made once per world at boot): `MeshLambertNodeMaterial({ map })` for road/curb/ground/supports with\n453\t  the baked textures (`NearestFilter`, repeat, sRGB) — this matches the engine's level look; walls/rails/props in\n454\t  `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ map, gradientMap: World3D.TOON_BANDS })` (E6). Track meshes are environment (not added to\n455\t  `world._obj`), so the \"objects\" filter mode leaves them alone. Cast/receive shadows on all track meshes.\n456\t- **Gates, boards, boost chevrons, edge poles, flags** are drawn with `world.pen` every frame (animated; a few dozen\n457\t  parts each). Gate posts also get box colliders.\n458\t- **Colliders** live in `TrackPhysics`'s own `RAPIER.World` (gravity irrelevant, never stepped with bodies; call\n459\t  `pw.step()` once after adding/removing colliders so queries see them). One trimesh collider per block (road + curbs\n460\t  + lips + underside) tagged with its surface(s) — if a block mixes surfaces (boost pad), split it into one collider\n461\t  per surface. Barriers/walls as separate trimesh colliders tagged `wall`. `Map(collider.handle → { blockId, surface })`.\n462\t- Rapier 0.19.3 JS facts verified while planning: `castRayAndGetNormal(ray, maxToi, solid, flags, groups,\n463\t  excludeCollider, excludeBody)` → `{ timeOfImpact, normal, collider, featureType, featureId }` (camelCase);\n464\t  `castShape(pos, rot, vel, shape, targetDistance, maxToi, stopAtPenetration, ...)` → `{ time_of_impact, witness1,\n465\t  witness2, normal1, normal2, collider }` (snake_case `time_of_impact`); `projectPoint(p, solid)` → `{ point,\n466\t  isInside, collider, ... }`; `intersectionsWithShape(pos, rot, shape, cb)`. Take a wall normal as `normal2` flipped,\n467\t  if needed, so it opposes the motion.\n468\t\n469\t### 4.4 The car (validated model — build exactly this, then tune with Dev Mode)\n470\t\n471\tThe car is **not** a physics body. Each step (1/120 s) it probes the track with four wheel rays, follows the surface\n472\tit's on with velocity-level constraints, and flies ballistically when the surface can't hold it. This is what makes\n473\tloops hold when you're fast and drop you when you're slow — TrackMania's feel — and it's deterministic.\n474\t\n475\tState (engine units): `p` (chassis reference point: centre of the wheelbase, `ride` above the road), `v`, frame\n476\t`f, u` (r derived), `grounded`, `n` (surface normal last step), `kappa` (filtered curvature), `slip` (detach timer),\n477\t`air` (seconds airborne), `flop` (crash-recovery timer), `boostT`, `drift`, `surface`, `blockId`, per-wheel `d` (for\n478\tdrawing suspension), `steerVis`, `slide` (for FX/sound), `impact` (last impact speed).\n479\t\n480\t```text\n481\tstep(car, ctl{steer -1..1, gas 0..1, brake 0..1}, dt):\n482\t  g = (0, 0, -G)                                   G = CFG.gravity (default 1.5 g = 14.7 m/s²)\n483\t  1 PROBE: for each wheel mount m_i = p + f·lx_i + r·ly_i (lx = ±wheelbase/2, ly = ±track/2):\n484\t       origin = m_i + u·0.5 m; ray along −u, length 0.5 m + ride + reach (reach = 0.9 m)\n485\t       hit -> point_i, normal_i, d_i = toi − 0.5 m, surface_i (collider map)\n486\t  2 SURFACE: if 4 hits: n = normalize(cross(FR − RL, FL − RR)), flipped to agree with u   (plane through the\n487\t       contacts: smooth on a faceted mesh — averaging triangle normals made the car let go at the top of the loop)\n488\t     else if ≥ 2 hits: n = normalize(Σ normal_i); else no surface.  d̄ = mean d_i.\n489\t  3 CONTACT:\n490\t     if n:\n491\t       vn = v·n;  pull = g·n  (> 0: gravity pulls the car off this surface)\n492\t       if grounded: k0 = −((n − car.n)·v̂) / max(1, |v|·dt);  car.kappa += (k0 − car.kappa)·min(1, dt/0.04)\n493\t       else car.kappa = 0\n494\t       holds = pull <= (|v|² − vn²)·car.kappa + A              A = CFG.adhesion (0.35 g)\n495\t       car.slip = holds ? 0 : car.slip + dt\n496\t       stay = grounded ? (car.slip < 0.06 s && d̄ < ride + reach)\n497\t                       : (vn < 0 && d̄ < ride + 0.6·reach)        (landing)\n498\t     grounded = n && stay\n499\t  4 GROUNDED:\n500\t     if just landed: impact = −vn; if u·n < 0.3 -> FLOP (step 8); else v −= n·vn,\n501\t                     v *= 1 − clamp((impact − 6 m/s)·0.02, 0, 0.25); landing FX (dust ring, thump, shake ∝ impact)\n502\t     u = normalize(u + (n − u)·min(1, 30·dt));  f = normalize(f − u(f·u));  r = u × f\n503\t     v −= n(v·n)                                                  (no motion into or off the surface)\n504\t     s = v·f\n505\t     a = g − n(g·n)                                               (gravity along the surface: slopes matter)\n506\t     gas:   if s < vtop: a += f·gas·A0·(1 − (max(s,0)/vtop)^1.6)\n507\t     brake: if s > 0.5 m/s: a −= v̂·brake·B (clamp so v doesn't reverse this step)\n508\t            else: a −= f·brake·Arev while s > −vrev           (reverse)\n509\t     coast: if gas = 0: a −= v̂·1.2 m/s²;  rolling: a −= v̂·surface.roll;  aero: a −= v·cd·|v|"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800","uuid":"fe3bfba2-c691-48dd-90dd-9e8188bad387","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:39:15.811Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174810__xbd1c73/work/PLAN.md","content":"One `World3D` per environment lives for the whole session (switching environments = switching which world draws and\nsteps; worlds that aren't shown don't step). Loading a track rebuilds only the track's meshes and colliders (fast;\nmaterials are pre-made — never create a material after boot, it stalls a frame to build its shader).\n\nThe **track ground**: the World3D `terrain` provides the scenery outside the grid (dunes and mesas, farmland hills,\nsnowy mountains) and sits at **−60 m under the grid**; our own **base plate** mesh covers the grid at z = 0 with holes\nfor \"chasm\" cells, so a chasm shows a deep canyon whose walls are the terrain's rock strata (\"the crack\"). Set\n`game.particles.ground` to our ground query after each world switch so dust and sparks land on the plate, not 60 m\nbelow (check whether World3D re-assigns it; if it does, override after it).\n\n### 2.7 Build to a single file\n\n`tools/build.mjs`:\n1. esbuild `entryPoints: ['src/entry.js'], bundle: true, format: 'esm', minify: true, legalComments: 'inline'`, with\n   a tiny plugin that resolves `three`, `three/webgpu` → `engine/vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`,\n   `three/tsl` → `.../three.tsl.min.js`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat` → `engine/vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js`\n   (use a plugin `onResolve`; esbuild's `alias` option does not reliably take sub-paths like `three/webgpu`).\n2. Write the HTML: `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then **`<script>` + hub/session.js\n   verbatim**, then the viewport meta, `<title>CRACKMANIA</title>`, the hub metas (§5.7), a `<style>` (stage + two\n   canvases as the engine's 3D pages use: `#view` under `#screen`, `image-rendering: pixelated`, black background),\n   `</head><body>`, the engine boot splash markup (`E.bootSplash('CRACKMANIA')` — get the string at build time by\n   loading the 2D engine in Node), `<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>`,\n   `<script>` + the 2D engine `</script>`, `<script type=\"module\">` + the bundle `</script>`.\n3. Escape `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`. Expect ~5 MB. No network fetches at run time.\n4. The file must run from `file://` (inline module scripts and base64 WASM need no server) and from the hub's server.\n**Validated while planning:** exactly this pipeline (esbuild 0.25.12 with the resolve plugin, the 2D engine inline as\na classic script, one inline module with three + Rapier + the 3D engine + a test scene) produced a **4.47 MB** file\nthat booted from `file://` in Playwright 1.63's headless Chromium with **zero errors**: three fell back to **WebGL 2**\n(no WebGPU adapter headless), the terrain, sky, a pen-drawn outlined car with its shadow and the pixel HUD all drew.\nExpected console warnings: Rapier's \"deprecated parameters for the initialization function\", \"No available\nadapters\", \"WebGPURenderer: WebGPU is not available\" — not errors. **Fallback** if inlining ever breaks: keep the\nbundle for the 3D engine + game only and add an import map to jsDelivr\n(`https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js`, `.../three.tsl.min.js`,\n`https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs`) — TASK.md allows CDN deps.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Player experience and controls\n\n### 3.1 The core loop (must feel instant)\n\nTrack select → **flyover** (2.5 s camera sweep along the route with the track name; any button skips) → **3-2-1-GO**\n(start-gate lights, Monsieur Drapeau drops the flag, beeps, gibberish count) → drive → each checkpoint shows the split\nand the delta to your best (blue `-0.42` ahead, red `+0.31` behind) → **FINISH** (slow-mo 0.6 s, camera swings\nround) → results (time, delta, medal drop-in, coppers count-up) → **Retry is one button and takes under half a\nsecond.** In play: **Respawn** puts you at the last checkpoint, standing, the clock still running (TrackMania 2003's\nclassic respawn); **Restart** goes back to the start with a short 1-second \"READY... GO\". Both work at any moment,\nincluding mid-air. The ghost of your best run (translucent) races with you; Coco's ghost (the author time, generated\nby the bot) can be switched on in Settings.\n\n### 3.2 Input maps (`game.input` presets; prompts always from `game.input.label(action)` + our glyph sprites)\n\nRace (`RACE_INPUT`; analog via E4 `input.analog`):\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| steer | left stick X (analog), d-pad ←/→ | ←/→, A/D | `input.move()[0]`; keyboard steering ramps (see §4.4 `steerRamp`) |\n| gas | R2 (Pad7, analog), ✕ (Pad0) | ↑, W | analog value 0..1 |\n| brake / reverse | L2 (Pad6, analog), □ (Pad2) | ↓, S | brake + steer at speed = drift (§4.4) |\n| respawn (last CP) | ○ (Pad1) | Enter, Backspace | TrackMania habit |\n| restart (from start) | △ (Pad3) | Delete, R | |\n| camera (far → close → hood) | R1 (Pad5) | C (also 1/2/3 pick one) | |\n| ghost on/off | L1 (Pad4) | G | |\n| horn (just for fun: two-tone toot + gibberish \"beep beep\") | R3 (Pad11) | H | |\n| pause | Options (Pad9) | Esc, P | engine standard |\nMouse in races: none (racing doesn't aim); the cursor hides while driving and reappears on menus.\n\nMenus (engine `up/down/left/right/confirm/cancel` + ours): d-pad/left stick move focus; ✕/Enter/Space choose;\n○/Esc/Backspace back; L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs; △/Y \"secondary\" (e.g. ghost toggle on track select, reset on a\nslider); **mouse hover moves the focus, click chooses, wheel scrolls lists**. Repeat on hold (`input.repeat`).\n\nEditor (`EDIT_INPUT`, §5.4) and replay (`REPLAY_INPUT`, §5.8) have their own tables in their sections.\n\nRumble (`input.rumble`): wall hits (∝ impact), hard landings, boost pads (short), checkpoint (tiny), finish.\n\n**AI Hub reserved:** in a session, ignore Options (pause) while Create (Pad8) is held.\n\n### 3.3 10-foot UI rules (apply everywhere)\n\n- Buffer is `res: 'ps1', expand: true` (~427×240 on 16:9). Primary text: pixel font at **scale 2** (14 px of 240 ≈\n  6% of screen height). Secondary text scale 1, never the `tiny` font for anything the player must read. Big numbers\n  (timer, speed) use a custom chunky digit sprite at 2–3× (§7.6).\n- Tiles at least 96×64 buffer px, 8 px gaps, 12 px safe margins (and keep clear of the engine's top-right corner\n  buttons: `game.cornerArea()`).\n- One focused element always visible (accent border + 1.08× scale tween + a soft glow); spatial navigation; hover =\n  focus; click = choose. Every screen has a back path (○/Esc/right-click).\n- A **prompt bar** along the bottom of every screen: `[glyph] Select  [glyph] Back  [glyph] ...` — glyphs follow\n  `input.lastDevice` (PlayStation shapes by default; Xbox letters if the Settings \"Button symbols\" is Xbox; keycaps\n  for keyboard; mouse icons when the mouse was last used).\n- No dense data screens: at most 6 numbers on any results/records panel; lists show 5–7 rows at scale 2 with\n  scrolling.\n- Colour is never the only signal (deltas carry `+`/`−`, medals carry letters B/S/G/A on their icons).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Core systems\n\n### 4.1 Blocks (the catalogue)\n\nEvery track — campaign, puzzle, editor — is a list of placements `{ type, cx, cy, L, rot }` (rot = heading of the\nblock's local +x: 0 E, 1 S, 2 W, 3 N). In its **local frame** a block's entry is the centre of the west face of local\ncell (0,0) at level 0, heading +x; local y is to the right (south when rot = 0). Footprint offsets below are\n`(along, right, levels)`.\n\n| Type | Token | Footprint (along × right × levels) | Exit (local cell, heading) | Geometry | Surface | Cost (¢) | Envs |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Start | `ST` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) → +x | straight; spawn at s = 8 m; start arch at s = 10 m | road | 0 (max 1) | all |\n| Finish | `FN` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) → +x | straight; finish arch at s = 16 m | road | 0 (≥1) | all |\n| Checkpoint | `CP` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) | straight; gate at s = 16 m | road | 15 | all |\n| Straight | `S` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) | straight | road | 5 | all |\n| Boost | `BO` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) | straight + boost pad (s 10–22 m) | road+boost | 40 | all |\n| Turn, tight | `L1`/`R1` | 1×1×1 | side cell | quarter circle r = 16 m | road | 8 | all |\n| Turn, wide | `L2`/`R2` | 2×2×1 | (1, ±2) heading ±y | quarter circle r = 48 m | road | 15 | all |\n| Turn, sweeping | `L3`/`R3` | 3×3×1 | (2, ±3) heading ±y | quarter circle r = 80 m | road | 25 | all |\n| Banked turn | `BL2`/`BR2` | 2×2×2 | as L2/R2 | r = 48 m, bank 0→28°→0 (sin ramp) | road | 30 | Dustbowl, Frostpeak |\n| Hill up/down (steep) | `U1`/`D1` | 1×1×2 | (1,0), level ±1 | smoothstep rise 8 m over 32 m (max grade 37.5%: launches you at speed) | road | 10 | all |\n| Hill up/down | `U2`/`D2` | 2×1×2 | (2,0), level ±1 | smoothstep rise 8 m over 64 m (max 18.75%) | road | 15 | all |\n| Kicker | `KI` | 1×1×2 | none (air) | concave arc to a **15° lip, 4.2 m high** at s = 32 m (arc radius 123.6 m) | road | 30 | all |\n| Landing | `LA` | 2×1×2 | (2,0) | air entry; smoothstep descent from +3 m to 0 over 64 m | road | 20 | all |\n| Gap | `GAPn` | n×1×2 reserved air | — | nothing (a reserved corridor); at level 0 the base plate is cut: **chasm** | — | 0 | all |\n| Loop | `LOOPR`/`LOOPL` | 2×2×4 | (2, ±1) → +x | §4.2: 12 m-radius loop with a one-cell sideways shift; lane assist | road | 120 | all |\n| Crossing | `CR` | 1×1×1 | all four faces | flat square deck, 4 ports | road | 20 | all |\n| Bumps | `BU` | 1×1×1 | (1,0) | three 0.5 m humps (`z = 0.5·sin²(3πs/32)`) | road | 10 | Hedgerow |\n| Dirt straight / turns | `DS`, `DL1`/`DR1`, `DL2`/`DR2` | as S / L1 / L2 | | | dirt | as base | Hedgerow, Dustbowl |\n| Ice straight / turns | `IS`, `IL1`/`IR1`, `IL2`/`IR2` | as S / L1 / L2 | | | ice | as base | Frostpeak |\n| Snow road variants | `SN` (straight) | as S | | | snow | 5 | Frostpeak |\n| Deco items | (editor only) | 1×1 | — | scenery props from §7.4 | — | 5–25 | per env |\n\nTurtle rules (§6.1) for where the next block goes follow from the \"Exit\" column: a turn of size n fills the n×n square\n`c0 + a·h + b·s` (a, b ∈ [0, n)) and exits into `c0 + (n−1)·h + n·s` heading `s` (s = the turn side); a loop fills a\n2×2×4 box and exits into `c0 + 2h + s` with the heading unchanged; hills occupy their cells on both levels they span;\na kicker reserves the level above its cell; `GAPn` reserves n cells on levels L and L+1.\n\n**Ports and links.** A port = (world cell, face, level, heading). Two blocks connect when a port of one and a port of\nthe other share the face, level, and opposite headings. Straight-like blocks are symmetric (drivable both ways); turns,\nhills, kickers, landings, loops, start and finish are directional for the route (a car can still drive them\nbackwards physically). **Jump links:** a kicker links to the first landing (or any block whose entry faces it) found\nalong its heading within 5 cells, on the kicker's level or up to 3 levels lower. The **route** is the chain of links\nfrom Start to Finish (§4.8); the editor shows unlinked ports as red arrows.\n\n### 4.2 Block paths (centrelines and frames)\n\nEach block type exports `path(s) -> { p, t, u }` for s ∈ [0, len] in local metres (p position, t unit tangent, u unit\nup), plus `width(s)`, `bank`, and `surface(s)`. Right is `r = u × t`. Formulas (local frame, entry at (0,0,0), +x\nforward, +y right):\n\n- **Straight** (n cells): `p = (s, 0, 0)`, `t = (1,0,0)`, `u = (0,0,1)`, `len = 32n`.\n- **Turn R, radius ρ = 32n − 16** (n = 1, 2, 3): `φ = s/ρ`, `p = (ρ sin φ, ρ(1 − cos φ), 0)`, `t = (cos φ, sin φ, 0)`,\n  `len = πρ/2`. L mirrors y. Check: n = 1 exits at (16, 16) — the centre of the south face of cell (0,0).\n- **Banked**: as the turn; bank `β(φ) = 28°·sin(π·φ/(π/2))`; `u = cos β·ẑ + sin β·r̂_in` where `r̂_in` points to the\n  turn centre (right for R). The road edge on the outside rises; the ports stay flat.\n- **Hill Un** (rise H = 8 m over len = 32n): `z(s) = H·(3x² − 2x³)`, x = s/len; `t ∝ (1, 0, z'(s))`;\n  `u ∝ (−z'(s), 0, 1)`. D mirrors (descends). The U1 crest has radius ≈ 21 m: above ~60 km/h the car takes off (by\n  design); U2's crest (≈ 85 m) holds the car up to ~150 km/h.\n- **Kicker**: `z(s) = ρk − sqrt(ρk² − s²)`, ρk = 123.6 m, s ∈ [0, 32]: lip angle 15°, lip height 4.2 m.\n- **Landing**: `z(s) = 3·(1 − (3x² − 2x³))`, x = s/64.\n- **Loop R** (exit one cell right, two cells ahead, same heading). Let `S = 32 + 2π·12 + 32` (≈ 139.4 m) be the\n  total centreline length and `y(s) = 32·(3q² − 2q³)` with q = s/S (the sideways shift spread over the whole piece).\n  For s ∈ [0, 32]: `p = (s, y, 0)`; for the circle (θ = (s − 32)/12 ∈ [0, 2π]): `p = (32 + 12 sin θ, y, 12(1 − cos θ))`,\n  `u0 = (−sin θ, 0, cos θ)`; for s ∈ [32 + 24π, S]: `p = (32 + (s − 32 − 24π), y, 0)` — i.e. the circle's bottom is at\n  local x = 32, entering and leaving there, shifted sideways. Tangent = normalized derivative (numerical, h = 0.01 m);\n  up = `u0` Gram–Schmidt'ed against t. At the circle's bottom points the entry and exit lanes are ~23 m apart, more\n  than a road width, so the ribbons never touch. L mirrors y. The loop block has **lane assist** (§4.4 step 7).\n- **Crossing**: a flat 32×32 m deck; its \"path\" for routing is the straight line through the two opposite ports the\n  route uses.\n\nPort frames must match exactly between linked blocks (position within 1 cm, tangent and up within 0.5°): a test checks\nevery link of every shipped track (§9).\n\n### 4.3 Geometry, materials and colliders\n\n**Ribbon mesher.** Sample each block path every 1 m (curves) or per cell (straights; 4 segments), and at each sample\nlay the environment's **cross-section** (a list of (lateral offset, height, uv.u, part) points) along `r` and `u`.\nParts: `road`, `line` (baked into the road texture), `curb`, `rail` (vertical barrier), `bank` (snowbank / hedge /\nstone wall), `underside`. Output per part: render triangles (three winding) + UVs (u across in metres/2, v along in\nmetres/4 so the pixel textures tile at ~8–12 cm per texel) and collision triangles (Rapier winding, engine units) with a\nsurface tag.\n\n| Env | Road width | Curb | Elevated edge | Ground-level edge | Supports under elevated pieces |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Dustbowl | 14 m asphalt, yellow centre dashes, white edges | 1 m red/white, +0.2 m, drivable | steel guard rail 0.9 m (solid) | sand (base plate) | concrete pillars 1.4 m square every 16 m along both edges |\n| Hedgerow | 12 m tarmac (or dirt), white edges | 0.8 m white/green | low stone wall 0.8 m (solid) | grass verge; hedges on some pieces (deco) | earth embankment (45° grassy slopes, drivable, slow) |\n| Frostpeak | 12 m packed-snow road (or ice), orange edge poles every 8 m (deco) | 0.8 m blue/white | snowbank 1.2 m (sloped, low grip: pushes you back) | snow | rock-and-snow embankment |\n\n- Turns and banked turns get the elevated-edge barrier on their **outer** edge even at ground level (Dustbowl: tyre\n  wall instead of rail).\n- Loops get 0.5 m lips both sides. Kickers and landings get curbs only. Gaps get nothing.\n- **Base plate** (per track): one mesh of the grid's cells minus chasm cells (GAP cells on level 0); environment ground\n  texture (sand ripples / mown grass stripes / snow with sparkles). Collider: one trimesh, surface = the env's\n  off-road surface. **Chasm walls**: for each chasm cell edge that borders plate, a vertical quad down 60 m with the\n  terrain rock texture. **Boundary**: a 2 m wall all round the grid (Dustbowl: striped concrete barriers; Hedgerow: dry\n  stone wall; Frostpeak: snow fence) with a collider.\n- **Materials** (made once per world at boot): `MeshLambertNodeMaterial({ map })` for road/curb/ground/supports with\n  the baked textures (`NearestFilter`, repeat, sRGB) — this matches the engine's level look; walls/rails/props in\n  `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ map, gradientMap: World3D.TOON_BANDS })` (E6). Track meshes are environment (not added to\n  `world._obj`), so the \"objects\" filter mode leaves them alone. Cast/receive shadows on all track meshes.\n- **Gates, boards, boost chevrons, edge poles, flags** are drawn with `world.pen` every frame (animated; a few dozen\n  parts each). Gate posts also get box colliders.\n- **Colliders** live in `TrackPhysics`'s own `RAPIER.World` (gravity irrelevant, never stepped with bodies; call\n  `pw.step()` once after adding/removing colliders so queries see them). One trimesh collider per block (road + curbs\n  + lips + underside) tagged with its surface(s) — if a block mixes surfaces (boost pad), split it into one collider\n  per surface. Barriers/walls as separate trimesh colliders tagged `wall`. `Map(collider.handle → { blockId, surface })`.\n- Rapier 0.19.3 JS facts verified while planning: `castRayAndGetNormal(ray, maxToi, solid, flags, groups,\n  excludeCollider, excludeBody)` → `{ timeOfImpact, normal, collider, featureType, featureId }` (camelCase);\n  `castShape(pos, rot, vel, shape, targetDistance, maxToi, stopAtPenetration, ...)` → `{ time_of_impact, witness1,\n  witness2, normal1, normal2, collider }` (snake_case `time_of_impact`); `projectPoint(p, solid)` → `{ point,\n  isInside, collider, ... }`; `intersectionsWithShape(pos, rot, shape, cb)`. Take a wall normal as `normal2` flipped,\n  if needed, so it opposes the motion.\n\n### 4.4 The car (validated model — build exactly this, then tune with Dev Mode)\n\nThe car is **not** a physics body. Each step (1/120 s) it probes the track with four wheel rays, follows the surface\nit's on with velocity-level constraints, and flies ballistically when the surface can't hold it. This is what makes\nloops hold when you're fast and drop you when you're slow — TrackMania's feel — and it's deterministic.\n\nState (engine units): `p` (chassis reference point: centre of the wheelbase, `ride` above the road), `v`, frame\n`f, u` (r derived), `grounded`, `n` (surface normal last step), `kappa` (filtered curvature), `slip` (detach timer),\n`air` (seconds airborne), `flop` (crash-recovery timer), `boostT`, `drift`, `surface`, `blockId`, per-wheel `d` (for\ndrawing suspension), `steerVis`, `slide` (for FX/sound), `impact` (last impact speed).\n\n```text\nstep(car, ctl{steer -1..1, gas 0..1, brake 0..1}, dt):\n  g = (0, 0, -G)                                   G = CFG.gravity (default 1.5 g = 14.7 m/s²)\n  1 PROBE: for each wheel mount m_i = p + f·lx_i + r·ly_i (lx = ±wheelbase/2, ly = ±track/2):\n       origin = m_i + u·0.5 m; ray along −u, length 0.5 m + ride + reach (reach = 0.9 m)\n       hit -> point_i, normal_i, d_i = toi − 0.5 m, surface_i (collider map)\n  2 SURFACE: if 4 hits: n = normalize(cross(FR − RL, FL − RR)), flipped to agree with u   (plane through the\n       contacts: smooth on a faceted mesh — averaging triangle normals made the car let go at the top of the loop)\n     else if ≥ 2 hits: n = normalize(Σ normal_i); else no surface.  d̄ = mean d_i.\n  3 CONTACT:\n     if n:\n       vn = v·n;  pull = g·n  (> 0: gravity pulls the car off this surface)\n       if grounded: k0 = −((n − car.n)·v̂) / max(1, |v|·dt);  car.kappa += (k0 − car.kappa)·min(1, dt/0.04)\n       else car.kappa = 0\n       holds = pull <= (|v|² − vn²)·car.kappa + A              A = CFG.adhesion (0.35 g)\n       car.slip = holds ? 0 : car.slip + dt\n       stay = grounded ? (car.slip < 0.06 s && d̄ < ride + reach)\n                       : (vn < 0 && d̄ < ride + 0.6·reach)        (landing)\n     grounded = n && stay\n  4 GROUNDED:\n     if just landed: impact = −vn; if u·n < 0.3 -> FLOP (step 8); else v −= n·vn,\n                     v *= 1 − clamp((impact − 6 m/s)·0.02, 0, 0.25); landing FX (dust ring, thump, shake ∝ impact)\n     u = normalize(u + (n − u)·min(1, 30·dt));  f = normalize(f − u(f·u));  r = u × f\n     v −= n(v·n)                                                  (no motion into or off the surface)\n     s = v·f\n     a = g − n(g·n)                                               (gravity along the surface: slopes matter)\n     gas:   if s < vtop: a += f·gas·A0·(1 − (max(s,0)/vtop)^1.6)\n     brake: if s > 0.5 m/s: a −= v̂·brake·B (clamp so v doesn't reverse this step)\n            else: a −= f·brake·Arev while s > −vrev           (reverse)\n     coast: if gas = 0: a −= v̂·1.2 m/s²;  rolling: a −= v̂·surface.roll;  aero: a −= v·cd·|v|","numLines":250,"startLine":260,"totalLines":1416}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019UFp3Y2EYnjMghe8GG8XeM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
